Guide.content._register("w16", {"id":"w16","order":16,"region":"operate","title":"Cost, Scale and Reliability","short":"Cost and reliability","tagline":"The bill and the outage arrive together. Plan for both.","story":"The assistant works, so traffic grows, and two things arrive on the same day: a finance review of the token bill and a 429 storm that nobody planned for. Cost, capacity and reliability pull on each other. Caching and routing cut spend, quotas shape what you can send, and every retry or failover has a price in tokens, latency or stale state.","oneLiner":"Find where the tokens go, buy capacity that fits the traffic shape, and fail in ways you chose in advance.","youWillLearn":["Trace where tokens are spent and rank the levers: caching, routing, output caps, compression and batch","Tell the three kinds of Azure 429 apart and explain why a 429 can appear below quota","Enforce limits per consumer at an API Management AI gateway with token limits and token metrics","Compare Standard, Priority, provisioned (PTU), Batch and Flex, and size PTUs from a traffic profile","Choose retries, circuit breakers, hedging, fallbacks, regional failover or degradation by failure type","Write SLIs and SLOs for an LLM application and plan capacity against them"],"minutes":105,"prereqs":["w02"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w16-fc-001","front":"List the main places an LLM app spends tokens.","back":"System prompt and tool definitions on every call, retrieved context, re-sent conversation history, output including hidden reasoning tokens, agent loops with tool results, and retries.","tags":["cost"]},{"id":"w16-fc-002","front":"Which cost metric beats cost per token for an agent, and why?","back":"Cost per successful task. A cheaper model can need more steps, retries or longer outputs, so per-token price hides the real cost.","tags":["cost"]},{"id":"w16-fc-003","front":"Why does a naive chat that re-sends full history cost grow faster than linearly?","back":"Input at turn k carries all earlier turns, so cumulative input grows with the square of the turn count. Twenty 500-token turns re-send 105,000 tokens.","tags":["cost"]},{"id":"w16-fc-004","front":"What did FrugalGPT report for LLM cascades?","back":"A cascade matched the best single LLM with up to 98% lower cost, or beat GPT-4 by 4% at equal cost, on 2023 benchmarks and prices.","tags":["routing"]},{"id":"w16-fc-005","front":"What are the Azure model router modes and defaults?","back":"`Balanced` (default, cheapest within about 1% to 2% of best quality), `Cost` (about 5% to 6%) and `Quality`. Version 2025-11-18 updates in place.","tags":["routing"]},{"id":"w16-fc-006","front":"Name the three causes of an Azure OpenAI 429.","back":"Quota exceeded, system capacity throttling, and a temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard. Compare `x-ratelimit-limit-tokens` with your assigned TPM to spot the third.","tags":["quota"]},{"id":"w16-fc-007","front":"How does Azure's TPM check treat `max_tokens`?","back":"It counts prompt plus `max_tokens` (plus `best_of`) up front. A `max_tokens` of 4,000 consumes 4,000 tokens of limit even for a 200-token answer.","tags":["quota"]},{"id":"w16-fc-008","front":"How does Anthropic's OTPM treat `max_tokens`?","back":"It counts actual output only, so a high `max_tokens` costs nothing against OTPM. Cache reads also do not count toward ITPM for most models.","tags":["quota"]},{"id":"w16-fc-009","front":"What status codes does APIM `llm-token-limit` return?","back":"429 when the per-minute rate is exceeded and 403 when the token quota is exceeded.","tags":["gateway"]},{"id":"w16-fc-010","front":"What does `estimate-prompt-tokens` do in `llm-token-limit`?","back":"It is required. `true` estimates prompt tokens in advance from the request schema. `false` uses actual usage from the model response. Streaming always estimates.","tags":["gateway"]},{"id":"w16-fc-011","front":"State the PTU sizing formula.","back":"`Normalized TPM = peak RPM x prompt tokens x (1 - cache rate) + output-to-input ratio x peak RPM x response tokens`. PTUs = that / input TPM per PTU, rounded up to the increment.","tags":["ptu"]},{"id":"w16-fc-012","front":"Why is PTU not always cheaper at scale?","back":"Capacity is held and billed whether or not requests arrive. Quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity, and spiky or small traffic leaves capacity idle.","tags":["ptu"]},{"id":"w16-fc-013","front":"What is PTU spillover?","back":"Overflow that returns a non-200 such as 429 is routed to a standard deployment in the same resource, for all requests or per request with `x-ms-spillover-deployment`.","tags":["ptu"]},{"id":"w16-fc-014","front":"What do Batch deployments trade for a 50% discount?","back":"A 24-hour completion target, no real-time SLA and a separate enqueued-token quota. They suit evaluation, enrichment and other work nobody waits for.","tags":["capacity"]},{"id":"w16-fc-015","front":"How is Azure Flex processing different from Batch?","back":"Per-request `service_tier: \"flex\"` with a synchronous API at about 50% off, but no SLA. It is preemptible, so 429 can mean no capacity and 408 means raise the timeout. Preview.","tags":["capacity"]},{"id":"w16-fc-016","front":"What is Azure Priority processing's latency promise?","back":"A target stated as p50 per 5-minute window, for example 99% above 80 tokens per second for some models. It is a target, not a per-request guarantee. It shares quota with standard.","tags":["capacity"]},{"id":"w16-fc-017","front":"List the OpenAI guidance for retrying 429s.","back":"Treat `Retry-After` as a minimum plus a small random delay, otherwise use exponential backoff with jitter, cap attempts and total time, and never retry quota or billing errors.","tags":["resilience"]},{"id":"w16-fc-018","front":"Why add jitter to exponential backoff?","back":"Capped backoff alone leaves clients retrying in clusters. Jitter spreads retries in time and reduces the total number of calls.","tags":["resilience"]},{"id":"w16-fc-019","front":"When do hedged requests pay off, and what did The Tail at Scale report?","back":"For cheap, idempotent calls with long tails: send a duplicate after about the p95 delay and take the first reply. A BigTable benchmark cut p99.9 from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for about 2% extra requests.","tags":["resilience"]},{"id":"w16-fc-020","front":"What does an APIM circuit breaker return when tripped?","back":"A 503. Rules are `failureCondition`, `tripDuration` and `acceptRetryAfter`. Rules are approximate across gateway instances and unavailable in the Consumption tier.","tags":["resilience"]},{"id":"w16-fc-021","front":"Does Foundry fail over between regions automatically?","back":"No. Build two resources in two regions. Approximate RTO is minutes for hot/hot, 30 minutes to 2 hours for hot/warm and 2 to 8 hours for hot/cold.","tags":["resilience"]},{"id":"w16-t01-fc1","front":"On GPT-5.6 and later, what do cache writes and reads cost relative to uncached input?","back":"Write 1.25x, read 0.1x (0.05x on GPT-6.1 Sol). One write plus nine reads is 2.15x against 10x uncached.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t01"},{"id":"w16-t01-fc2","front":"Why can a one-word answer be expensive?","back":"Reasoning tokens count inside completion tokens. The hidden generation is billed even when the visible answer is short.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t01"},{"id":"w16-t01-fc3","front":"Which metric hides retries and agent loops?","back":"Cost per token. Track cost per successful task and tokens per task instead.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t01"},{"id":"w16-t02-fc1","front":"When does a 1.25x write plus 0.1x reads beat uncached?","back":"From the second request inside the lifetime: 1.25 + 0.1(N-1) against N. One use loses 25%.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t02"},{"id":"w16-t02-fc2","front":"Which saves more latency: halving output or halving input?","back":"Output, roughly half the latency by OpenAI's rule of thumb. Halving input may save only 1% to 5% of latency, though it still cuts cost.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t02"},{"id":"w16-t02-fc3","front":"What does a semantic cache cost on a miss?","back":"An embedding call plus a vector lookup before the normal model call. Net savings need a high hit rate.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t02"},{"id":"w16-t03-fc1","front":"Router versus cascade?","back":"A router picks a model before the call. A cascade calls the cheap model first, checks the result and escalates on failure.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t03"},{"id":"w16-t03-fc2","front":"What are the Azure model router modes?","back":"Balanced (default, within about 1% to 2% of best quality), Cost (about 5% to 6%) and Quality.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t03"},{"id":"w16-t03-fc3","front":"Two hidden costs of routing?","back":"It splits the prompt cache across models, and the router's context window equals the smallest model in the pool.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t03"},{"id":"w16-t04-fc1","front":"What are Azure's three causes of 429?","back":"Quota exceeded, system capacity throttling, and a temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard. Compare the limit header with assigned TPM.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t04"},{"id":"w16-t04-fc2","front":"Why can `max_tokens: 4000` cause 429s?","back":"Azure and OpenAI count it toward the limit up front, so each call reserves 4,000 tokens whatever it generates.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t04"},{"id":"w16-t04-fc3","front":"What does `llm-token-limit` return when exceeded?","back":"429 for the rate limit and 403 for the quota. Counters are per gateway, and streaming always estimates.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t04"},{"id":"w16-t05-fc1","front":"Does PTU quota guarantee capacity?","back":"No. Quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity. Create the deployment first.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t05"},{"id":"w16-t05-fc2","front":"How does spillover work?","back":"Non-200 overflow from a full PTU deployment goes to a standard deployment in the same resource, for all requests or per request via `x-ms-spillover-deployment`.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t05"},{"id":"w16-t05-fc3","front":"What is Priority processing's latency target?","back":"A p50 token rate per 5-minute window by model, for example 80 TPS for gpt-5.6-sol. It is a target, not a per-request guarantee.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t05"},{"id":"w16-t06-fc1","front":"What do Batch and Flex give and take?","back":"About 50% off in exchange for time. Batch has a 24-hour target. Flex is synchronous but preemptible with no SLA.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t06"},{"id":"w16-t06-fc2","front":"Why can Flex hurt production?","back":"It shares quota with Standard and has no automatic fallback, so a surge or a 429 needs your own handling.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t06"},{"id":"w16-t06-fc3","front":"Why does Batch need `custom_id`?","back":"Results are not guaranteed to return in input order. The id joins outputs to inputs and drives replay of failures.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t06"},{"id":"w16-t07-fc1","front":"What do `llm-token-limit` return on breach?","back":"429 for the tokens-per-minute rate, 403 for the token quota. Counters are per gateway, not aggregated across regions.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t07"},{"id":"w16-t07-fc2","front":"What happens when a backend breaker trips?","back":"The gateway returns 503. Rules are approximate across instances and unavailable in the Consumption tier. Use `acceptRetryAfter` for long Retry-After values.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t07"},{"id":"w16-t07-fc3","front":"What APIM metric limits exist?","back":"5 custom dimensions per policy, 100 unique values per dimension, 1,000 active time series per namespace. Beyond that data is dropped silently.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t07"},{"id":"w16-t08-fc1","front":"What is full jitter?","back":"Sleep a random time between 0 and min(cap, base x 2^attempt). It spreads retries and cuts total calls versus no jitter.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t08"},{"id":"w16-t08-fc2","front":"Why retry at only one layer?","back":"Retries multiply across layers. Three retries at five layers is 3^5 = 243 times the load on the bottom service.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t08"},{"id":"w16-t08-fc3","front":"How should `Retry-After` be used?","back":"As a minimum wait, plus a small random delay. Never retry quota or billing errors.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t08"},{"id":"w16-t09-fc1","front":"What are the breaker states?","back":"Closed (count failures), open (reject fast), half-open (probe with a few calls). APIM returns 503 when open.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t09"},{"id":"w16-t09-fc2","front":"Bulkhead in one line?","back":"Partition capacity into pools so one consumer's fault or load stays inside its own pool.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t09"},{"id":"w16-t09-fc3","front":"When is hedging safe?","back":"Cheap, idempotent, short calls with spare capacity. Hedge after about p95, cap the hedge rate and cancel the loser.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t09"},{"id":"w16-t10-fc1","front":"Fallback versus graceful degradation?","back":"A fallback swaps in another component of the same kind. Degradation keeps a reduced function, such as read-only mode or retrieval-only answers.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t10"},{"id":"w16-t10-fc2","front":"What must users be told in degraded mode?","back":"What still works and what changed. Azure's reliability guidance calls for a notification mechanism.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t10"},{"id":"w16-t10-fc3","front":"Why tag degraded responses?","back":"So dashboards and SLOs count them separately. Otherwise a green success rate hides worse answers.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t10"},{"id":"w16-t11-fc1","front":"Does Foundry fail over automatically?","back":"No. Build two resources in two regions with two project connections, plus Service Health alerts and availability tests.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t11"},{"id":"w16-t11-fc2","front":"Error budget for 99.9% over 30 days?","back":"43.2 minutes. A 40-minute outage burns about 93% of it.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t11"},{"id":"w16-t11-fc3","front":"What does failover cost that a drill may hide?","back":"A cold prompt cache, secondary quota and capacity that may be sized for tests, and state that lived in the primary region.","tags":[],"topic":"w16-t11"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"AI gateway capabilities in Azure API Management","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/genai-gateway-capabilities","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Map of token limits, metrics, semantic caching, load balancing and circuit breaker. The Foundry unified model API and AI gateway in Foundry are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"llm-token-limit policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-token-limit-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token rate limit and quota attributes, estimation rules and caveats.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"llm-emit-token-metric policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-emit-token-metric-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token metrics to Application Insights and the cardinality limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Backends in Azure API Management","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/backends","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Circuit breaker rules and load-balanced pools.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r5","title":"Deployment types for Microsoft Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/deployment-types","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Global, Data Zone, Regional, Provisioned, Batch and Developer SKUs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"What is provisioned throughput for Foundry Models?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/provisioned-throughput","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"PTU model, quota versus capacity, reservations and spillover.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r7","title":"Determine PTU sizing for a workload","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/provisioned-throughput-sizing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The sizing formula with a worked example and spillover header.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r8","title":"Manage Azure OpenAI quota in Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/quota","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"TPM and RPM quota, rate-limit headers and the three causes of 429.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"Azure OpenAI 429 and token rate limits (Dev Proxy)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-cloud/dev/dev-proxy/concepts/azure-openai-429-token-rate-limit","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How max_tokens counts against TPM and why 429s appear below quota.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r10","title":"Priority processing for Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/priority-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Pay-as-you-go tier with a p50 latency target per 5-minute window.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"Flex processing (Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/flex-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Preview tier: 50% discount, no SLA, preemptible capacity.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r12","title":"Batch API (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/batch","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"50% discount, 24-hour window, file format and limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r13","title":"Batch processing (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Message Batches limits, expiry and result retention.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r14","title":"Model router for Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-router","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Routing modes, supported deployment types, limits and the caching interaction.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"High availability and resiliency for Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/how-to/high-availability-resiliency","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"No automatic failover, hot/hot, hot/warm and hot/cold recovery targets, Front Door with APIM.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"Rate limits (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/rate-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Limit types, tiers and retry guidance with backoff and jitter.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"Rate limits (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Token bucket, ITPM and OTPM, acceleration limits and cache-aware limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Exponential Backoff And Jitter","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/","type":"blog","publisher":"AWS Architecture Blog","note":"Why capped backoff alone leaves retry clusters and how jitter fixes it.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"The Tail at Scale","url":"https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/","type":"paper","publisher":"Google Research (Communications of the ACM, 2013)","note":"Latency tail causes and hedged requests.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05176","type":"paper","publisher":"Stanford","note":"LLM cascades and the up to 98% cost reduction result.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r21","title":"RouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18665","type":"paper","publisher":"UC Berkeley and others","note":"Preference-trained routers that cut cost with little quality loss.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"LLMLingua: Compressing Prompts for Accelerated Inference of Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05736","type":"paper","publisher":"Microsoft Research","note":"Prompt compression up to 20x with little loss on listed tasks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Access Foundry Models and other language models through a gateway","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/azure-openai-gateway-guide","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Gateway pattern across the Well-Architected pillars.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r24","title":"Manage your generative AI APIs with Azure API Management (Learn module)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/api-management/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Study aid: hands-on gateway policies.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"Prompt caching (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Write and read multipliers that set the caching break-even.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r26","title":"Prompt caching (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Multipliers for 5-minute and 1-hour caching that stack with batch pricing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Performance and latency (Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/latency","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Latency formula and the Azure Monitor metrics used for latency SLIs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"llm-semantic-cache-lookup policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-semantic-cache-lookup-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Paired lookup and store policies, score-threshold guidance and the warning about incorrect, outdated or unsafe hits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Tutorial: Use Azure Managed Redis as a semantic cache","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/redis/tutorial-semantic-cache","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How to partition and version a semantic cache and tune thresholds and TTL.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter (Amazon Builders' Library)","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/","type":"blog","publisher":"AWS","note":"Retry amplification across layers (243x), single-point retries, token-bucket retry limits, idempotency, timeout choice.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Circuit Breaker pattern (Azure Architecture Center)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Closed, open and half-open states and why half-open limits probe traffic.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Bulkhead pattern (Azure Architecture Center)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/patterns/bulkhead","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Partitioning consumers and services into pools so one fault does not cascade.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Recommendations for self-preservation and self-healing (Azure Well-Architected, Reliability)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/well-architected/reliability/self-preservation","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Graceful degradation, automated failure detection and notifying users of degraded functionality.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Service Level Objectives (Google SRE book)","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/","type":"doc","publisher":"Google","note":"SLIs, SLOs, percentiles over means and the error budget idea.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Latency optimization (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Output tokens versus input tokens as a latency lever, and the seven principles.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr9","title":"Flex processing (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/flex-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Beta status, Batch API rates with cache discounts and the 15-minute timeout example.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Azure OpenAI reasoning models in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens and reported in completion_tokens_details or output_tokens_details.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr11","title":"Prompt caching in Foundry Models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"PTU-M keeps prompt caching but has no cache breakpoints and no cache_write_tokens.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"TPM / RPM","def":"Tokens per minute and requests per minute. The two quota dimensions that most LLM endpoints enforce, each per model and deployment or per organization."},{"term":"429","def":"HTTP status for too many requests. Can mean quota exceeded, capacity throttling or a temporary limit cut. Often carries `Retry-After` or `retry-after-ms`."},{"term":"PTU","def":"Provisioned throughput unit. Reserved model capacity billed whether or not requests arrive, with its own quota and sizing rules."},{"term":"Spillover","def":"Routing overflow from a provisioned deployment to a standard deployment in the same resource when the provisioned one returns a non-200 such as 429."},{"term":"Priority processing","def":"A pay-as-you-go tier with a latency target stated as p50 per 5-minute window. Not a per-request guarantee."},{"term":"Flex processing","def":"A preview per-request tier at roughly Batch prices on a synchronous API, with no SLA and preemptible capacity."},{"term":"Batch API","def":"Asynchronous processing at about 50% off with a 24-hour completion target. For work nobody is waiting on."},{"term":"Model router","def":"A single endpoint that picks an underlying model per request, trading cost against quality. Azure offers Balanced, Cost and Quality modes."},{"term":"Cascade","def":"Try a cheap model first and escalate to a stronger one when a check says the answer is not good enough."},{"term":"Jitter","def":"Random delay added to a backoff interval so many clients do not retry at the same moment."},{"term":"Circuit breaker","def":"A rule that stops sending traffic to a failing backend for a trip duration. In APIM a tripped backend returns 503."},{"term":"Hedged request","def":"A duplicate request sent after a delay near the p95 latency, taking whichever reply arrives first. Cuts the tail at the cost of extra load."},{"term":"RTO / RPO","def":"Recovery time objective and recovery point objective: how long recovery may take and how much data loss is tolerable."},{"term":"Error budget","def":"The allowed unreliability implied by an SLO, for example 43.2 minutes per 30 days at 99.9%."},{"term":"AI gateway","def":"A front door for model traffic that applies token limits, metrics, caching, safety and routing. In Azure it is built on API Management."},{"term":"Graceful degradation","def":"Serving a reduced but acceptable result, such as a smaller model, a shorter answer or a cached one, when full capacity is not available."},{"term":"Bulkhead","def":"A partition of capacity into pools so that a fault or heavy consumer in one pool cannot exhaust capacity for the others."},{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"A client-supplied token that lets a server recognise a repeated request and apply its side effect only once, which makes retries safe."},{"term":"Token bucket","def":"A rate-limit model where tokens refill at a steady rate and each request spends tokens. It allows bursts up to the bucket size and then limits the sustained rate."},{"term":"Cache write","def":"The first request that stores a prompt prefix in a provider cache. On GPT-5.6 and later and on Anthropic it is billed above the normal input rate (1.25x for the short lifetime)."},{"term":"Normalized TPM","def":"The load figure in the PTU sizing formula: prompt tokens after caching plus output tokens weighted by the output-to-input ratio, summed over peak requests per minute."},{"term":"SLI and SLO","def":"A service level indicator is a measured signal such as TTFT p95 or success rate. A service level objective is the target for it, and the allowed miss rate is the error budget."},{"term":"Load shedding","def":"Deliberately rejecting or degrading low-priority work when capacity is exhausted so that important work keeps succeeding."},{"term":"Enqueued-token quota","def":"A separate quota that caps the input tokens waiting in a Batch queue, so batch work does not disturb online deployments."},{"term":"Cold cache","def":"A prompt cache with no entries for your prefix, as in a freshly used failover region. The first requests pay full input prices and higher time to first token."}],"levels":[{"id":"w16-l1","order":1,"title":"Follow the tokens","kind":"concept","minutes":15,"brief":"Finance says the model bill doubled while traffic grew 20 percent. Before you cut anything, find where the tokens go and which lever fits. Prices, model mix, prompt shapes and the amount of agent looping are all unknown.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Where tokens are spent","level":2,"id":"where-tokens-are-spent"},{"t":"table","caption":"Six token sinks, how to see them and what moves them","head":["Sink","Why it grows","How you see it","Levers"],"rows":[["**System prompt and tool definitions**","Sent on every call, and each added tool adds tokens","Prompt tokens split by section","Prompt caching (reads at 0.1x), fewer and shorter tool definitions"],["**Retrieved context**","Top-k times chunk size on every call","Tokens per retrieval step","Fewer, better chunks, reranking, prompt compression"],["**Conversation history**","Re-sent every turn, so cumulative input grows with the square of turns","Input tokens by turn index","Append-only history with a cached prefix, rare compaction"],["**Output and reasoning tokens**","Billed at the output rate and set latency. Hidden reasoning counts in completion tokens","`reasoning_tokens` in usage details","Output caps, stop sequences, reasoning effort, concise formats"],["**Agent loops and tool results**","Each step re-sends context plus the previous tool output","Calls per task, tokens per task","Step and token budgets, smaller contexts for sub-agents"],["**Retries and duplicates**","Every layer that retries repeats the whole call","Requests per unique task id","Attempt caps, one retry layer, idempotency keys"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"formula","title":"Cost per successful task","expr":"cost_per_task = calls_per_task x (uncached_in x p_in + cached_in x p_cached + written_in x p_write + out x p_out) / success_rate","vars":[{"k":"calls_per_task","v":"model calls per task, which agent loops multiply"},{"k":"p_cached, p_write","v":"cached read and cache write rates, for example 0.1x and 1.25x of p_in on GPT-5.6 and later"},{"k":"out","v":"output tokens including hidden reasoning tokens"},{"k":"success_rate","v":"share of tasks that finish correctly. A cheaper model with a lower rate costs more per success"}],"example":"A chat that re-sends full history across 20 turns of 500 tokens each sends 500 x (1 + 2 + ... + 20) = **105,000 input tokens** for 10,000 new ones. With an append-only prefix cached at 0.1x read and 1.25x write on the new tokens, the bill is about 95,000 x 0.1 + 10,000 x 1.25 = **22,000 token-equivalents**, roughly 79% less [[r25]]. Compaction or truncation rewrites the prefix and resets that saving."},{"t":"compare","title":"Four ways to pay less per request","intro":"Dials: spread of query difficulty, quality of your eval set, how the cache interacts, latency of escalation and residency of the routed models.","options":[{"name":"Static rules by task","summary":"Send classification to a small model and reasoning-heavy work to a large one, by task type you define.","pros":["Predictable and easy to audit","No router overhead"],"cons":["Rules drift as models change","Misses difficulty variation inside a task type"],"bestWhen":"Clear task types and a small team.","avoidWhen":"Difficulty varies widely inside one task type.","meta":[{"k":"Effort","v":"low"},{"k":"Risk","v":"rule drift"}]},{"name":"Trained router","tag":"Azure model router: GA","summary":"One deployment that picks a model per request. Azure's `Balanced` mode (default) targets the cheapest model within about 1% to 2% of the best quality, `Cost` within about 5% to 6%, and `Quality` favors accuracy [[r14]].","pros":["One endpoint for mixed difficulty","RouteLLM-style routers cut cost by more than 2x in some cases with no quality loss [[r21]]"],"cons":["Splits the prompt-cache prefix across models, so caching helps only when consecutive requests reach the same model","Context window equals the smallest underlying model, and routing reads text only","Current version 2025-11-18 updates in place, so the routed pool can change under you"],"bestWhen":"Mixed difficulty and an eval set you can compare against a fixed baseline.","avoidWhen":"Uniformly hard tasks, or a strict residency or model pin.","meta":[{"k":"Deployment types","v":"Global Standard, Data Zone Standard"}]},{"name":"Cascade","summary":"Try a cheap model, check the answer, and escalate to a stronger model only when the check fails.","pros":["FrugalGPT matched the best single LLM with up to 98% lower cost, or beat GPT-4 by 4% at equal cost (2023 prices) [[r20]]"],"cons":["Escalation adds latency and doubles spend on hard queries","Only as good as the verifier"],"bestWhen":"The cheap model is right most of the time and a reliable check exists.","avoidWhen":"Most queries are hard, or no trustworthy verifier exists.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"adds a step on escalation"}]},{"name":"Prompt compression","summary":"Shrink long inputs before the call. LLMLingua reports up to 20x compression with little loss on GSM8K, BBH, ShareGPT and Arxiv-March23 [[r22]].","pros":["Cuts input tokens on very long, unique contexts"],"cons":["Quality risk on exact quotations","The compression step itself costs compute","Breaks a stable cached prefix"],"bestWhen":"Very long unique inputs and cost-bound batch work.","avoidWhen":"A stable prefix that caching already handles, or text that must be quoted exactly.","meta":[{"k":"Evidence","v":"2023 benchmarks"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w16-l1-lever","title":"Pick the first cost lever","prompt":"Flip the prompt shape, the difficulty spread and the timing. The best first lever changes.","dials":[{"id":"shape","label":"Prompt shape","values":[{"id":"stable","label":"Long stable prefix"},{"id":"unique","label":"Mostly unique long context"}]},{"id":"diff","label":"Difficulty","values":[{"id":"mixed","label":"Mixed, many easy requests"},{"id":"uniform","label":"Uniformly hard"}]},{"id":"timing","label":"Timing","values":[{"id":"live","label":"Interactive"},{"id":"async","label":"Nobody is waiting"}]}],"options":[{"id":"cache","name":"Prompt caching","summary":"Read the stable prefix at a fraction of the price."},{"id":"route","name":"Router or cascade","summary":"Send easy requests to cheaper models."},{"id":"batch","name":"Batch or Flex","summary":"Half price for delay-tolerant work."},{"id":"outcap","name":"Output caps and concise format","summary":"Cut output and reasoning tokens."},{"id":"compress","name":"Prompt compression","summary":"Shrink long unique inputs."}],"scores":{"cache":{"shape":{"stable":{"s":2,"why":"Reads at 0.1x beat uncached after two requests."},"unique":{"s":-1,"why":"Writes at 1.25x are never read back."}},"diff":{"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"Works the same whatever the difficulty."},"uniform":{"s":0,"why":"Works the same whatever the difficulty."}},"timing":{"live":{"s":1,"why":"It also lowers time to first token."},"async":{"s":0,"why":"Saves cost only."}}},"route":{"shape":{"stable":{"s":-1,"why":"Splits the cached prefix across models."},"unique":{"s":0,"why":"No cache to lose."}},"diff":{"mixed":{"s":2,"why":"Easy requests go to cheap models, so most spend drops."},"uniform":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing to route, and you pay router overhead."}},"timing":{"live":{"s":-1,"why":"Escalation adds latency on hard queries."},"async":{"s":1,"why":"Escalation latency is hidden."}}},"batch":{"shape":{"stable":{"s":1,"why":"Discounts can stack with cached-token pricing, for example on Anthropic."},"unique":{"s":0,"why":"The 50% discount does not depend on prompt shape."}},"diff":{"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of difficulty."},"uniform":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of difficulty."}},"timing":{"live":{"s":-2,"why":"A 24-hour target and no real-time SLA."},"async":{"s":2,"why":"About 50% off for work nobody waits for."}}},"outcap":{"shape":{"stable":{"s":0,"why":"Output limits do not touch the input side."},"unique":{"s":0,"why":"Output limits do not touch the input side."}},"diff":{"mixed":{"s":1,"why":"Easy requests rarely need long answers."},"uniform":{"s":1,"why":"Hidden reasoning tokens can dominate hard tasks."}},"timing":{"live":{"s":1,"why":"Output tokens drive latency as well as cost."},"async":{"s":1,"why":"Output is billed at the higher rate."}}},"compress":{"shape":{"stable":{"s":-1,"why":"Compressing breaks the stable prefix that caching reuses."},"unique":{"s":2,"why":"Long unique context is where compression pays."}},"diff":{"mixed":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of difficulty."},"uniform":{"s":0,"why":"Independent of difficulty."}},"timing":{"live":{"s":-1,"why":"The compression step adds compute and delay."},"async":{"s":0,"why":"Delay is tolerable."}}}},"takeaway":"Start with the lever that matches the shape of the spend. Caching for stable prefixes, routing for mixed difficulty, batch for delay-tolerant work, caps for output, compression for long unique inputs."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Batch and Flex discount","value":"about 50%","note":"24-hour target for Batch. Flex has no SLA"},{"label":"Cache read and write","value":"0.1x and 1.25x","note":"OpenAI GPT-5.6 and later, Anthropic 5-minute"},{"label":"Cascade evidence","value":"up to 98%","note":"FrugalGPT, 2023 benchmarks and prices"},{"label":"Compression evidence","value":"up to 20x","note":"LLMLingua, little loss on listed tasks"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Say this out loud","md":"Measure cost per successful task, not per token. A cheaper model can need more retries, longer outputs or more agent steps. Attribute tokens by consumer and by section of the prompt before you choose a lever, and say which lever the spend shape points to."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-l1-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A chat re-sends the whole history each turn. Each turn adds 500 tokens, so turn k sends 500 x k input tokens. How many input tokens are sent across 20 turns?","answer":105000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"500 x (1 + 2 + ... + 20)","explain":"The sum 1 + 2 + ... + 20 is 210, so 500 x 210 = 105,000 input tokens. Only 10,000 of them are new content. The rest is re-reading, which is why an append-only prefix with caching, or compaction, changes the bill so much.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r25"},{"id":"w16-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A nightly job scores 2 million documents against a long, shared instruction prompt. Nobody waits for the result, and a 24-hour turnaround is fine. Which approach lowers cost most?","options":["Priority processing, because it gives the lowest latency for a very large evaluation run","A semantic cache across all documents, because they share one prefix","Batch with the shared instruction prefix first, accepting the 24-hour target","Provisioned throughput sized for the nightly peak and billed hourly, for stable speed"],"answer":2,"explain":"Batch gives about 50% off for delay-tolerant work, and a stable prefix first can still earn cached-token pricing. Priority pays a premium for latency nobody needs. Documents differ, so a semantic cache would rarely match and could return the wrong document's answer. Provisioned capacity stays billed while idle, which is wasteful for a nightly job.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r12"},{"id":"w16-l1-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Tokens per task for an agent doubled after a release. Select every plausible driver.","options":["Conversation history is re-sent on every model call","Tool results are appended to the context at each step","Hidden reasoning tokens count in the completion tokens","A shorter system prompt was shipped in the same release"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"History, tool output and reasoning tokens all grow tokens per task, especially in loops. A shorter system prompt reduces tokens, so it cannot explain a doubling. Check calls per task and output tokens by step to see which one moved.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r25"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r25","r26","r12","r14","r20","r21","r22"],"takeaways":["Attribute tokens by sink and consumer before choosing a lever","Cost per successful task is the metric. Cost per token hides retries, loops and failures","History re-sent every turn grows with the square of the turn count, so keep the prefix append-only and cached","Routers split the prompt-cache prefix across models, so measure caching and routing together","Batch is the boring, large win for work nobody waits for"]},{"id":"w16-l2","order":2,"title":"Decode the 429","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"Requests start failing with 429 while dashboards show spare quota. Which kind of limit you hit, how the provider counts tokens and where you enforce limits for many consumers are all open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three different 429s on Azure","level":2,"id":"three-different-429s-on-azure"},{"t":"table","caption":"Azure documents several 429 causes; these three need different fixes [[r8]]","head":["Cause","How you recognize it","What to do"],"rows":[["**Quota exceeded**","`x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` or `-requests` reaches 0 and your own usage is at the assigned TPM","Reduce consumption, raise the assignment, add capacity, or spill over to another deployment"],["**System capacity throttling**","429s while you are under quota, usually short-lived, with `retry-after-ms`","Retry with backoff and jitter, add a second deployment or region, consider provisioned capacity"],["**Temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard**","`x-ratelimit-limit-tokens` is lower than your assigned TPM","Compare the header with the assignment. A quota increase does not fix it, so back off and add headroom elsewhere"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"table","caption":"How each provider counts, as of 2026-10-13","head":["Dimension","Azure OpenAI in Foundry","OpenAI","Anthropic"],"rows":[["**Limit units**","TPM per subscription, region, model and deployment type. RPM follows TPM: 6 per 1,000 TPM for older chat models, 1 per 1,000 for o3 and o4-mini","RPM, RPD, TPM, TPD and IPM per organization and project, per model, by usage tier","Token bucket with continuous refill: RPM, ITPM and OTPM per model class, plus acceleration limits for sharp ramps"],["**`max_tokens` accounting**","Prompt plus `max_tokens` plus `best_of` counts up front, so `max_tokens: 4000` reserves 4,000 tokens even for a 200-token answer","Counts the larger of `max_tokens` and a character-based estimate","OTPM counts actual output only, so a high `max_tokens` costs nothing"],["**Window and signals**","RPM enforced over 1 or 10 second windows. Headers `x-ratelimit-limit-requests`, `x-ratelimit-limit-tokens`, `x-ratelimit-remaining-*`, `x-ratelimit-reset-*`. 429 adds `retry-after-ms`","429 `slow_down` and 503 `server_is_overloaded`, which may carry `Retry-After`","Cache reads do not count toward ITPM for most models. At an 80% hit rate, 2,000,000 ITPM handles 10,000,000 input tokens per minute"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"h","text":"Put the limit at the gateway","level":2,"id":"put-the-limit-at-the-gateway"},{"t":"table","caption":"Where to enforce a limit","head":["Option","Scope","Strength","Weakness"],"rows":[["**App-side limiter**","One process or service","Sheds load before the call and costs nothing","No view across replicas or consumers"],["**APIM `llm-token-limit`**","Per `counter-key`: caller, subscription or product","Token-aware rate and quota in one place for many consumers, with attribution","Per-gateway counters, estimates and brief overshoot, covered below"],["**Provider or deployment quota**","Per deployment","Protects the model deployment from overload","Shared by every consumer, so one noisy consumer starves the others"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-l2-gateway","title":"Policy order at an AI gateway","alt":"Consumers call an API Management gateway. The gateway applies a per-key token limit, then a semantic cache lookup, then forwards to a backend pool with two deployments. The gateway also emits token metrics to Application Insights.","src":"flowchart LR\n  C[Consumers] --> G[APIM gateway]\n  G --> L[Token limit per key]\n  L --> S[Semantic cache lookup]\n  S --> P[Backend pool]\n  P --> A[Deployment A]\n  P --> B[Deployment B]\n  G --> M[Token metrics]\n  M --> I[App Insights]","caption":"Limit first so a cold cache cannot overload the backend. The AI gateway covers token limits, token metrics, semantic caching, load balancing and circuit breaking [[r1]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"xml","title":"Per-consumer token rate, monthly quota and token metrics","code":"<policies>\n  <inbound>\n    <base />\n    <llm-token-limit\n        counter-key=\"@(context.Subscription.Id)\"\n        tokens-per-minute=\"50000\"\n        token-quota=\"5000000\"\n        token-quota-period=\"Monthly\"\n        estimate-prompt-tokens=\"true\" />\n    <llm-emit-token-metric namespace=\"llm-usage\">\n      <dimension name=\"Subscription ID\" />\n      <dimension name=\"API ID\" />\n    </llm-emit-token-metric>\n  </inbound>\n  <outbound>\n    <base />\n  </outbound>\n</policies>","caption":"Attributes from the policy reference [[r2]] [[r3]]. A rate overrun returns 429 and a quota overrun returns 403, so clients must handle both differently. `estimate-prompt-tokens` is required: `true` estimates prompt tokens before the call, which can reject an over-limit request before it reaches the backend, and `false` uses actual usage from the response."},{"t":"failure","title":"Gateway gotchas that cause surprise 429s and bad dashboards","items":[{"symptom":"A consumer exceeds its limit for a moment","cause":"Concurrent requests can briefly exceed the token limit, and counters are per gateway, not aggregated across regions","fix":"Leave headroom below the provider limit, and set per-region limits instead of one global number"},{"symptom":"Image-heavy prompts are rejected early","cause":"When estimating, each image counts up to 1,200 tokens, and streaming requests always estimate","fix":"Raise limits for vision consumers or meter them separately, and check estimates against real usage"},{"symptom":"A token metric silently stops appearing","cause":"More than 5 custom dimensions per policy, 100 unique values per dimension or 1,000 active time series per namespace. Excess data is discarded without an error","fix":"Keep dimensions low-cardinality, such as subscription, API and model. Never use user id or request id"},{"symptom":"Streamed answers show wrong token counts","cause":"Some OpenAI models omit usage when streaming, and an interrupted stream gives inaccurate counts","fix":"Set `include_usage: true` on streaming requests and treat interrupted streams as approximate"}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A Standard deployment has an assigned 120,000 TPM. Your own metrics show about 19,000 tokens used in the last minute, yet calls return the response below. Which cause fits best?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests\nx-ratelimit-limit-tokens: 20000\nx-ratelimit-remaining-tokens: 0\nretry-after-ms: 8000"},"options":["Azure temporarily cut the effective limit on this Standard deployment below the assignment","You exceeded your assigned quota, so the fix is to request a higher TPM assignment for the model","The deployment is provisioned and its PTU pool is exhausted, so spillover should handle it","`max_tokens` is set too low, which makes the service reject requests before they run"],"answer":0,"explain":"The header says the effective limit is 20,000, while 120,000 is assigned, which is the third documented 429 cause. A higher assignment would not change a temporary reduction. A provisioned deployment would not use the Standard headers this way. A low `max_tokens` does not cause rejections.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r8"},{"id":"w16-l2-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A service sends 120 requests per minute to an Azure deployment. Each prompt is 1,500 tokens and each request sets `max_tokens` to 4,000 although answers average 300 tokens. How many tokens per minute does the limiter count?","answer":660000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens per minute","hint":"Azure counts prompt plus max_tokens up front","explain":"Each request counts 1,500 + 4,000 = 5,500 tokens, so 120 x 5,500 = 660,000. Real usage is only 120 x 1,800 = 216,000. Lowering `max_tokens` toward the real answer length frees most of the limit.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r9"},{"id":"w16-l2-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every true statement about the APIM `llm-token-limit` policy.","options":["A rate overrun returns 429 and a quota overrun returns 403","Counters are per gateway and are not aggregated across regions","Streaming requests always use estimated prompt tokens","Concurrent requests can never push a consumer over its limit"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The first three come from the policy reference. The last is false: concurrent requests can briefly exceed the limit, so leave headroom and do not treat the policy as a hard billing cap.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w16-l2-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A team moves a workload from Azure OpenAI to Anthropic and lowers `max_tokens` from 4,000 to 400 to stay under limits. What is the effect on rate limiting?","options":["It halves 429s, because OTPM reserves the full `max_tokens` up front for every request","No limit benefit, because OTPM counts actual output, and answers may be cut off","It breaks caching, because `max_tokens` is part of the cache key","It lowers ITPM use, because input and output tokens share a single combined bucket"],"answer":1,"explain":"On Anthropic, OTPM counts actual output only, so a high `max_tokens` costs nothing, and lowering it only risks truncation. The Azure habit does not transfer. `max_tokens` is not part of a cache key, and ITPM and OTPM are separate limits.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r8","r9","r16","r17","r2","r3","r1","r23","r24"],"takeaways":["Azure has several kinds of 429, three of them quota, capacity throttling and a temporary limit cut. Read the headers first","A 429 can appear below quota. Oversized `max_tokens` and short RPM windows are common causes","Providers count differently. Anthropic OTPM ignores `max_tokens` and cache reads for most models","Enforce per-consumer limits at the gateway, and expect estimates, overshoot and per-gateway counters","Keep metric dimensions low-cardinality or data is dropped without an error"]},{"id":"w16-l3","order":3,"title":"Buy capacity that fits the traffic","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"You need model capacity and the invoice must make sense. Traffic may be steady or spiky, users may be waiting or not, and data may or may not be allowed to leave a region. Choose how to buy tokens, then size it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five ways to buy tokens on Azure","level":2,"id":"five-ways-to-buy-tokens-on-azure"},{"t":"compare","title":"Capacity and pricing modes","intro":"Dials: traffic predictability and burstiness, p95 latency need, residency boundary, interactive versus asynchronous, cache hit rate and the cost of idle reserved capacity. Prices change often, so use these as structure and plug in your own price sheet.","options":[{"name":"Global Standard","tag":"GA","summary":"Pay-as-you-go. Microsoft advises starting here: it launches first when a new model releases, has the lowest price and the broadest region coverage [[r5]].","pros":["No commitment and the highest quota","Newest models first"],"cons":["Prompts may be processed in any region, though data at rest stays in the chosen geography","Shared capacity, so latency varies at large steady volume"],"bestWhen":"Small or spiky traffic, flexible residency, the newest model.","avoidWhen":"A strict processing boundary or a tight p95 on large steady volume.","meta":[{"k":"SKU","v":"GlobalStandard"},{"k":"Commitment","v":"none"}]},{"name":"Priority processing","tag":"GA","summary":"A pay-as-you-go tier with a latency target stated as p50 per 5-minute window, for example 99% above 80 tokens per second for gpt-5.6-sol and gpt-6-sol, 70 for gpt-5.6-terra, 50 for gpt-5.5 and 100 for gpt-5.4-mini. Set on the deployment or per request with `service_tier` (`auto`, `default`, `priority`) [[r10]].","pros":["Latency help without a commitment","Uses the same quota as standard"],"cons":["A p50 target per window, not a per-request guarantee","Carries a price premium. Check the price sheet","Offered on Global Standard or Data Zone Standard US"],"bestWhen":"Bursty or business-hour interactive traffic that needs steadier latency.","avoidWhen":"Work nobody waits for, or a need for a contractual latency guarantee.","meta":[{"k":"Field","v":"service_tier: priority"}]},{"name":"Provisioned (PTU) with spillover","tag":"GA","summary":"Reserved capacity billed per PTU per hour, or through 1-month or 1-year Azure Reservations. Overflow that returns a non-200 such as 429 can spill to a standard deployment in the same resource [[r6]] [[r7]].","pros":["Stable latency at sustained volume","Cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity, so a high cache rate lowers the PTUs you need"],"cons":["Capacity is held and billed whether or not requests arrive","Quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity","PTU-M deployments keep caching but have no cache breakpoints and no `cache_write_tokens`"],"bestWhen":"Predictable, high, sustained volume with a latency target.","avoidWhen":"Small or spiky traffic, where idle capacity costs more than pay-as-you-go.","meta":[{"k":"Spillover","v":"x-ms-spillover-deployment per request"},{"k":"Not for","v":"Azure DeepSeek and Meta Llama spillover"}]},{"name":"Batch","tag":"GA","summary":"Azure Global Batch and Data Zone Batch cost 50% less than Global Standard, with a 24-hour target, a separate enqueued-token quota and no real-time SLA. OpenAI's Batch API is also 50% off with a `.jsonl` file, one model per file and a 200 MB upload limit [[r5]] [[r12]].","pros":["Half price","A separate quota keeps it from competing with live traffic"],"cons":["Not interactive at all","Results arrive within a 24-hour window, so downstream steps must be asynchronous"],"bestWhen":"Evaluation, enrichment, embeddings and classification where 24 hours is fine.","avoidWhen":"Anything a user is waiting for.","meta":[{"k":"SKU","v":"GlobalBatch, DataZoneBatch"}]},{"name":"Flex processing","tag":"Preview","summary":"Per-request `service_tier: \"flex\"` on Azure Global Standard at 50% off, with cached-token discounts also applying. It has no latency or service SLA, shares quota with Standard and does not fall back to Standard on its own [[r11]].","pros":["Batch-like price with a synchronous API shape"],"cons":["Capacity is preemptible, so 429 can mean no capacity","408 means raise the timeout (the docs use 900 s)","From 2026-09-25 unsupported models return 400"],"bestWhen":"Delay-tolerant work that still wants synchronous calls.","avoidWhen":"You cannot tolerate 429 or 408, or the model lacks Flex support.","meta":[{"k":"Status","v":"Preview, checked 2026-10-13"}]}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Deployment types and where prompts are processed [[r5]]","head":["Family","SKUs","Processing boundary"],"rows":[["**Global**","`GlobalStandard`, `GlobalProvisionedManaged`, `GlobalBatch`","Any region. Data at rest stays in the designated geography"],["**Data Zone**","`DataZoneStandard`, `DataZoneProvisionedManaged`, `DataZoneBatch`","Inside the US, EU or APAC data zone"],["**Regional**","`Standard`, `ProvisionedManaged` (Regional Provisioned)","Inside the chosen geography"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"formula","title":"PTU sizing","expr":"Normalized TPM = peak RPM x prompt tokens x (1 - cache rate) + output_input_ratio x peak RPM x response tokens;  PTUs = Normalized TPM / input TPM per PTU, rounded up to the model's increment","vars":[{"k":"output_input_ratio","v":"how many input-token equivalents one output token costs, a model constant"},{"k":"input TPM per PTU","v":"model-specific throughput per PTU. Minimum PTUs and ratios vary by model"},{"k":"cache rate","v":"share of prompt tokens served from cache. Cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity"}],"example":"The documented example (Data Zone Provisioned, 3,400 input TPM per PTU, ratio 8, increment 5): 1,000 RPM with a 200-token prompt and a 20-token response gives 1,000 x 200 + 8 x 1,000 x 20 = 360,000, so 105.88, rounded up to **110 PTUs**. At a 50% cache rate it is 260,000, so 76.47, rounded up to **80 PTUs** [[r7]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Doc example, no cache","value":"110 PTUs","note":"105.88 rounded up to an increment of 5"},{"label":"Doc example, 50% cache","value":"80 PTUs","note":"76.47 rounded up"},{"label":"Raw PTU reduction","value":"about 28%","note":"105.88 to 76.47, derived"},{"label":"Batch and Flex discount","value":"50%","note":"Batch has a 24-hour target. Flex is preview"}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w16-l3-capacity","title":"Choose a capacity model","prompt":"Flip the traffic shape, the latency need and the residency boundary.","dials":[{"id":"traffic","label":"Traffic shape","values":[{"id":"steady","label":"Steady and high"},{"id":"spiky","label":"Spiky or bursty"},{"id":"small","label":"Small and sporadic"}]},{"id":"latency","label":"Latency need","values":[{"id":"live","label":"Interactive, steady p95"},{"id":"late","label":"Tolerant, hours are fine"}]},{"id":"bound","label":"Residency","values":[{"id":"global","label":"Global processing is fine"},{"id":"zone","label":"Must stay in a zone or geography"}]}],"options":[{"id":"gs","name":"Global Standard","summary":"Pay as you go, global processing."},{"id":"dzs","name":"Data Zone Standard","summary":"Pay as you go inside a data zone."},{"id":"ptu","name":"Provisioned with spillover","summary":"Reserved capacity, overflow to standard."},{"id":"pri","name":"Priority processing","summary":"Pay-as-you-go with a latency target."},{"id":"bat","name":"Batch or Flex","summary":"Half price, delay tolerant."}],"scores":{"gs":{"traffic":{"steady":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, but latency can vary at large steady volume."},"spiky":{"s":1,"why":"You pay only for use and draw on large shared quota."},"small":{"s":1,"why":"No commitment and the lowest price."}},"latency":{"live":{"s":0,"why":"Shared capacity gives variable latency."},"late":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but Batch is cheaper for delay-tolerant work."}},"bound":{"global":{"s":2,"why":"Highest quota, lowest price and newest models first."},"zone":{"s":-2,"why":"Prompts can be processed outside the zone."}}},"dzs":{"traffic":{"steady":{"s":0,"why":"Same shape as Global Standard."},"spiky":{"s":1,"why":"Pay only for use."},"small":{"s":1,"why":"No commitment."}},"latency":{"live":{"s":0,"why":"Shared capacity, variable latency."},"late":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but Batch is cheaper."}},"bound":{"global":{"s":-1,"why":"You accept a boundary you do not need."},"zone":{"s":2,"why":"Processing stays inside the US, EU or APAC zone."}}},"ptu":{"traffic":{"steady":{"s":2,"why":"High utilization makes held capacity pay off."},"spiky":{"s":-2,"why":"Idle capacity is billed, and the peak overflows."},"small":{"s":-2,"why":"Minimum PTUs exceed what you need."}},"latency":{"live":{"s":2,"why":"Stable latency is the main benefit."},"late":{"s":-1,"why":"Reserved capacity sits idle for work that could wait."}},"bound":{"global":{"s":1,"why":"A global provisioned type exists."},"zone":{"s":1,"why":"Data Zone and Regional provisioned types exist, but capacity may be unavailable."}}},"pri":{"traffic":{"steady":{"s":1,"why":"Works, but a premium on all volume may cost more than PTU."},"spiky":{"s":2,"why":"You pay the premium only when traffic arrives."},"small":{"s":0,"why":"No commitment, but the premium adds up per token."}},"latency":{"live":{"s":2,"why":"A p50 target per 5-minute window, not a per-request guarantee."},"late":{"s":-2,"why":"A premium for latency nobody needs."}},"bound":{"global":{"s":1,"why":"Available on Global Standard."},"zone":{"s":0,"why":"Data Zone Standard US only, so check your zone."}}},"bat":{"traffic":{"steady":{"s":1,"why":"Large jobs fit a queue."},"spiky":{"s":1,"why":"The queue absorbs bursts."},"small":{"s":1,"why":"Half price on only what you use."}},"latency":{"live":{"s":-2,"why":"A 24-hour target and no real-time SLA."},"late":{"s":2,"why":"50% off for work nobody waits for."}},"bound":{"global":{"s":1,"why":"Global Batch is available."},"zone":{"s":1,"why":"Data Zone Batch exists."}}}},"takeaway":"There is no best mode in the abstract. Steady and interactive favors PTU, bursty and interactive favors Priority, delay-tolerant favors Batch, and a boundary favors the zone or regional types."},{"t":"failure","title":"Capacity mistakes and how you would notice","items":[{"symptom":"A 1-year reservation is purchased and the deployment will not create","cause":"Reservations and quota do not guarantee capacity","fix":"Create the deployment first, confirm capacity, then buy the reservation"},{"symptom":"High PTU bill with low utilization","cause":"Spiky or small traffic leaves held capacity idle","fix":"Size PTUs for the steady baseline and let spillover or pay-as-you-go absorb peaks. Watch `AzureOpenAIProvisionedManagedUtilizationV2`"},{"symptom":"429s on a provisioned deployment at peak","cause":"Peak exceeds provisioned capacity and no spillover is configured","fix":"Configure spillover to a standard deployment in the same resource, globally or per request. It does not support Azure DeepSeek or Meta Llama"},{"symptom":"Flex requests start failing with 400 after a model change","cause":"From 2026-09-25 unsupported models return 400, and Flex never falls back to Standard on its own","fix":"Check Flex support per model, handle 400, 408 and 429 explicitly and use a long client timeout"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview and price notes, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Flex processing is preview on Azure and beta at OpenAI. The Foundry unified model API and AI gateway in Foundry are preview. Price levels are not quoted here because they change. The break-even against pay-as-you-go is PTUs x hourly PTU price x hours versus expected tokens x token price. Reservations only lower the left side and do not guarantee capacity [[r6]] [[r11]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-l3-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A workload peaks at 600 requests per minute with 500-token prompts and 50-token responses and no caching. The model's output-to-input ratio is 4, input TPM per PTU is 2,500 and the PTU increment is 5. How many PTUs do you provision?","answer":170,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"PTUs","hint":"Normalized TPM / input TPM per PTU, rounded up to the increment","explain":"Normalized TPM = 600 x 500 + 4 x 600 x 50 = 300,000 + 120,000 = 420,000. Divided by 2,500 gives 168, and rounding up to the next multiple of 5 gives 170. A 40% cache rate would cut the input term to 180,000 and the result to 120 PTUs.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"},{"id":"w16-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Interactive traffic is steady all day but spikes to 10 times normal for 20 minutes daily. Which capacity plan fits best?","options":["Buy PTUs for the full 10x peak so latency stays stable all day, even while capacity sits idle","Move all interactive traffic to Batch, because the daily peak is short and the discount is 50%","Size PTUs for the steady baseline and spill the peak over to a standard deployment","Use Flex for the interactive traffic, because it costs half as much and answers synchronously"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sizing for the baseline keeps utilization high and spillover handles the short peak at pay-as-you-go prices. A peak-sized PTU purchase leaves most capacity idle. Batch has no real-time SLA. Flex is preemptible with no SLA, so it is wrong for users who are waiting.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"},{"id":"w16-l3-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every true statement about provisioned throughput (PTU).","options":["Capacity is held and billed whether or not requests arrive","Cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity","A reservation guarantees capacity in the region","Quota guarantees capacity","PTU quota is per subscription, region and deployment type"],"answers":[0,1,4],"explain":"Held billing, cached tokens and the quota scope are documented. Neither reservations nor quota guarantee capacity. That is why you create the deployment before buying a reservation.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"},{"id":"w16-l3-q4","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each mode to its contract.","pairs":[{"l":"Priority processing","r":"Latency target stated as p50 per 5-minute window, billed pay-as-you-go"},{"l":"Batch","r":"24-hour target, separate enqueued-token quota and 50% less"},{"l":"Flex (preview)","r":"Preemptible with no SLA, so 429 can mean no capacity"},{"l":"Provisioned","r":"Capacity held for you and billed hourly or through a reservation"}],"explain":"Each mode sells a different promise. Priority sells steadier latency without commitment, Batch sells a price for delay, Flex sells batch pricing on a synchronous API without guarantees, and PTU sells held capacity.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r5"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r5","r6","r7","r10","r11","r12","r13","r23"],"takeaways":["Start from the traffic shape: steady favors PTU, bursty favors Priority, delay-tolerant favors Batch","PTU sizing is a formula. A higher cache rate lowers the PTUs you need","Quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity, so create the deployment first","Spillover turns a provisioned primary plus standard overflow into one plan","Label Flex and the Foundry gateway items as preview, with the date you checked"]},{"id":"w16-l4","order":4,"title":"Fail well: retries, breakers, hedges and failover","kind":"problem","minutes":25,"brief":"Calls to the model fail in different ways: a blip, an exhausted quota, a slow tail, a dead region. Each deserves a different response, and the wrong one makes things worse. How critical the call is, whether it is cheap to repeat and how long recovery may take are all open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Match the pattern to the failure","level":2,"id":"match-the-pattern-to-the-failure"},{"t":"dials","id":"w16-l4-resilience","title":"Pick the resilience pattern","prompt":"Flip the failure type, the nature of the call and how critical it is.","dials":[{"id":"failure","label":"Failure type","values":[{"id":"blip","label":"Transient overload, short Retry-After"},{"id":"quota","label":"Sustained quota exhaustion"},{"id":"region","label":"Regional outage"},{"id":"tail","label":"Slow tail, no errors"}]},{"id":"call","label":"The call","values":[{"id":"cheap","label":"Cheap and idempotent"},{"id":"costly","label":"Expensive or stateful"}]},{"id":"need","label":"Criticality","values":[{"id":"critical","label":"Critical, must answer"},{"id":"soft","label":"Degradable, a reduced answer is fine"}]}],"options":[{"id":"retry","name":"Retry with backoff and jitter","summary":"Honor Retry-After, cap attempts."},{"id":"breaker","name":"Circuit breaker and backend pool","summary":"Trip a bad backend, fail over to a healthy one."},{"id":"region","name":"Multi-region active-active or hot/warm","summary":"A second region you built in advance."},{"id":"hedge","name":"Hedged requests","summary":"Duplicate after the p95 delay."},{"id":"degrade","name":"Degrade or shed","summary":"Smaller model, shorter answer, cache, queue."}],"scores":{"retry":{"failure":{"blip":{"s":2,"why":"Honor Retry-After, add jitter and cap attempts and total time."},"quota":{"s":-2,"why":"Retrying quota or billing errors never succeeds, so do not."},"region":{"s":-1,"why":"Retries pile onto a dead region."},"tail":{"s":0,"why":"Retries fire after a failure, not after slowness."}},"call":{"cheap":{"s":1,"why":"A repeat costs little and is safe."},"costly":{"s":-1,"why":"Repeats are expensive, and stateful calls may not be safe to repeat."}},"need":{"critical":{"s":1,"why":"Often enough to save the request."},"soft":{"s":0,"why":"A fallback may be a better use of the time."}}},"breaker":{"failure":{"blip":{"s":1,"why":"Trips after repeated failures and spreads load."},"quota":{"s":1,"why":"A pool shifts traffic to a deployment with headroom."},"region":{"s":2,"why":"Priority groups route to a healthy region while the breaker trips the bad one."},"tail":{"s":0,"why":"A breaker reacts to failures, not to slowness."}},"call":{"cheap":{"s":1,"why":"No state to move."},"costly":{"s":-1,"why":"Stateful APIs need session affinity behind a pool."}},"need":{"critical":{"s":1,"why":"Keeps a path to a working backend."},"soft":{"s":0,"why":"Useful but not decisive."}}},"region":{"failure":{"blip":{"s":0,"why":"Overkill for a blip."},"quota":{"s":1,"why":"A second region brings its own quota."},"region":{"s":2,"why":"The only pattern that survives a regional outage, and you build it yourself."},"tail":{"s":0,"why":"Does not address slowness."}},"call":{"cheap":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but the standby is a fixed cost."},"costly":{"s":-1,"why":"State and prompt caches are per region, so a failover starts cold."}},"need":{"critical":{"s":2,"why":"Recovery targets range from minutes to hours by tier."},"soft":{"s":-1,"why":"A standby costs more than degradation for non-critical work."}}},"hedge":{"failure":{"blip":{"s":0,"why":"A duplicate does not help a rejected request."},"quota":{"s":-2,"why":"Duplicates add load to an exhausted quota."},"region":{"s":0,"why":"Only helps if the duplicate can reach another region."},"tail":{"s":2,"why":"Cut p99.9 from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for about 2% more requests in the BigTable benchmark."}},"call":{"cheap":{"s":2,"why":"Cheap and idempotent is the safe case for duplicates."},"costly":{"s":-2,"why":"A duplicate long generation doubles real spend."}},"need":{"critical":{"s":1,"why":"Trims the tail on critical paths."},"soft":{"s":0,"why":"Rarely worth the load."}}},"degrade":{"failure":{"blip":{"s":0,"why":"Retry first."},"quota":{"s":2,"why":"Smaller model, shorter output, cached answer, queue or shed."},"region":{"s":1,"why":"Keeps the core flow alive on reduced capacity."},"tail":{"s":1,"why":"Cutting work fits the time budget."}},"call":{"cheap":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"costly":{"s":1,"why":"A cheaper fallback beats paying twice."}},"need":{"critical":{"s":-1,"why":"A degraded answer may break the guarantee."},"soft":{"s":2,"why":"The product decision is already made."}}}},"takeaway":"Name the failure first. Retries fix blips, degradation fixes sustained quota pressure, a second region fixes a regional outage and hedging fixes only the tail."},{"t":"table","caption":"Retry and hedging rules from the sources","head":["Technique","Rule","Source"],"rows":[["**Honor Retry-After**","Treat it as a minimum and add a small random delay","OpenAI rate limits [[r16]]"],["**Backoff with jitter**","Without a header, use exponential backoff with jitter. Capped backoff alone leaves retry clusters, and jitter spreads them and cuts total calls","OpenAI [[r16]] and AWS [[r18]]"],["**Caps**","Cap attempts and total time","OpenAI [[r16]]"],["**Never retry**","Quota and billing errors","OpenAI [[r16]]"],["**One retry layer**","Count SDK retries so nested loops do not multiply traffic","OpenAI [[r16]]"],["**Hedged requests**","After about the p95 delay send a duplicate and take the first reply. In a Google BigTable benchmark, p99.9 for 1,000 keys fell from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for about 2% extra requests","The Tail at Scale [[r19]]"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-l4-breaker","title":"What a circuit breaker does","alt":"State diagram. The breaker starts closed. When the failure count is reached in the interval it moves to open. After the trip duration, or the accepted Retry-After value, it returns to closed.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Closed\n  Closed --> Open: failure count reached in the interval\n  Open --> Closed: trip duration over or Retry-After passed","caption":"In APIM a tripped backend makes the gateway return 503 for traffic that would have gone to it [[r4]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"A backend circuit breaker rule (illustrative)","code":"\"circuitBreaker\": {\n  \"rules\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"openAiBreaker\",\n      \"failureCondition\": {\n        \"count\": 3,\n        \"interval\": \"PT1M\",\n        \"statusCodeRanges\": [ { \"min\": 429, \"max\": 429 }, { \"min\": 500, \"max\": 599 } ]\n      },\n      \"tripDuration\": \"PT1M\",\n      \"acceptRetryAfter\": true\n    }\n  ]\n}","caption":"A backend has one `circuitBreaker` rule with `failureCondition`, `tripDuration` and `acceptRetryAfter`. Rules are approximate across gateway instances and are not in the Consumption tier. For Azure OpenAI backends accept `Retry-After`, because it can be large, for example a day. Pools hold up to 30 backends with round-robin (default), weighted or priority balancing, and lower priority groups are used only when higher ones are tripped [[r4]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Regional recovery tiers. Foundry does not fail over automatically, so you build two resources in two regions with two project connections [[r15]]","head":["Strategy","Approximate RTO","Approximate RPO","Relative cost"],"rows":[["**Hot/hot**","Minutes","Near zero","Highest: full duplicate resources in both regions"],["**Hot/warm**","30 minutes to 2 hours","Minutes to hours","Moderate: critical resources running, others on standby"],["**Hot/cold**","2 to 8 hours","Hours","Lowest: provisioned but not active"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of the failure handling","items":[{"symptom":"A short blip becomes a long outage","cause":"A retry storm: SDK, application and gateway each retry, so three layers with three retries each send up to 4 x 4 x 4 = 64 attempts per request","fix":"Keep one retry layer, cap attempts and total time, add jitter and a breaker. Detect with requests per unique task id"},{"symptom":"A breaker never opens for one failing gateway instance","cause":"Rules are approximate because gateway instances do not synchronize","fix":"Treat it as best effort and watch backend failure rates centrally"},{"symptom":"Clients see 503 and blame the model","cause":"A tripped breaker makes the gateway itself return 503","fix":"Tag gateway-originated errors in logs and traces and alert on breaker-open time"},{"symptom":"Failover works on paper and fails in the incident","cause":"The second region has no quota, deployment or model version, and Foundry does not fail over for you","fix":"Create both deployments in advance, add Service Health alerts and availability tests, and rehearse. Front Door with one APIM per region can route around a dead region"},{"symptom":"Cost and p95 TTFT jump after failover","cause":"Prompt caches are not shared across regions, so the standby starts cold","fix":"Prewarm where possible and budget for the cold start"},{"symptom":"Stateful conversations break when traffic moves","cause":"Stateful APIs such as Responses need session affinity behind a backend pool","fix":"Enable session awareness on the pool, or keep conversation state outside the model API"}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-l4-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the handling of a failed model call in order, following OpenAI's retry guidance.","items":["Classify the error: do not retry quota or billing errors","Wait at least the Retry-After value, otherwise use exponential backoff","Add a small random jitter to the delay","Stop at the attempt cap or the total time budget","Fall back or degrade instead of retrying again"],"explain":"Classification comes first because some errors can never succeed. The wait honors the server's hint as a minimum. Jitter prevents synchronized retries. Caps bound the cost, and a fallback is the exit when the budget runs out.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w16-l4-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"An SDK, an application wrapper and a gateway each make one attempt plus 3 retries. When the backend keeps failing, how many calls can reach the backend for one user request?","answer":64,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"calls","hint":"Each layer multiplies: (1 + 3) per layer","explain":"Each layer makes 4 attempts per attempt of the layer above, so 4 x 4 x 4 = 64. This is why OpenAI advises counting SDK retries, and why one retry layer with a cap and jitter is the safer design.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w16-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which hedging policy fits an LLM workload best?","options":["Hedge every call after 50 ms to cut the median, since duplicates cost less than slow answers","Hedge long streaming generations, because they have the worst tails and duplicates add little load","Never hedge LLM calls, because a duplicate can only make the tail worse under load","Hedge cheap idempotent calls after about the p95 delay, cap the extra spend and cancel the loser"],"answer":3,"explain":"Hedging works when the duplicate is cheap and safe, fires after about the p95 delay and has a spend cap. Hedging at 50 ms doubles load without helping the tail. Duplicating long generations doubles the real cost. Never hedging ignores the large p99.9 gains reported for cheap calls.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r19"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r4","r15","r16","r18","r19","r1","r23"],"takeaways":["Name the failure, then pick the pattern. Retries for blips, degradation for quota pressure, a second region for outages, hedging for tails","Never retry quota or billing errors, and keep to one retry layer with jitter and caps","A tripped APIM breaker returns 503 from the gateway, so label gateway errors in your traces","Foundry does not fail over for you. Build, test and rehearse the second region","Failover starts with a cold prompt cache and may lack quota, so plan for cost and latency"]},{"id":"w16-l5","order":5,"title":"Boss: stay in budget through a regional incident","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer says token spend doubled last quarter, and last week a regional incident took the assistant offline for 40 minutes. Keep it inside budget and available. Traffic shape, latency needs, residency and recovery targets are all unspecified.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"SLOs and error budgets for an LLM application","level":2,"id":"slos-and-error-budgets-for-an-llm-application"},{"t":"table","caption":"Author synthesis, not a vendor rule. Pair every latency number with token counts","head":["Category","SLI","How to measure","Dials that change the target"],"rows":[["**Latency**","TTFT p95 for streaming UIs","Azure Monitor `AzureOpenAITimeToResponse`, or first-byte time from your traces","Interactive versus batch, user tolerance for a first-token delay"],["**Latency**","TTLT p95, bucketed by output tokens","`AzureOpenAITTLTInMS` with `GeneratedTokens`","Output length and reasoning effort"],["**Reliability**","Success rate excluding client quota errors, plus 429 and 503 rates","Gateway logs and backend metrics","Whether a degraded answer counts as success"],["**Reliability**","Breaker-open time and failover success","Gateway metrics and rehearsal results","Recovery tier: hot/hot, hot/warm or hot/cold"],["**Efficiency**","Cache hit ratio, cost per successful task, tokens per task","`cached_tokens` over prompt tokens, `llm-emit-token-metric` by consumer","Prefix stability, routing, agent loop depth"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-l5-topology","title":"A budget-aware, region-tolerant topology","alt":"Clients reach Azure Front Door, which routes to two API Management instances in different regions. Region A has a PTU deployment that spills over to a standard deployment, and a batch lane for asynchronous work. Region B has a standard deployment.","src":"flowchart TB\n  U[Clients] --> F[Front Door]\n  F --> A[APIM region A]\n  F --> B[APIM region B]\n  A --> P[PTU deployment A]\n  P -- spillover --> S[Standard deployment A]\n  B --> D[Standard deployment B]\n  A --> Q[Batch lane for async work]","caption":"Baseline on PTU, bursts on standard through spillover, asynchronous work on Batch, and a second region built in advance. Front Door with one APIM per region can route around a dead region [[r15]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Capacity planning loop","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Measure","md":"Peak and average RPM, prompt and output token distributions, cache rate and the interactive versus async split per consumer."},{"title":"Split","md":"Baseline on provisioned capacity, bursts on Priority or Standard, asynchronous work on Batch."},{"title":"Size","md":"Apply the PTU formula with a measured cache rate. Add failover headroom in the surviving region."},{"title":"Limit","md":"Per-consumer token limits and quotas at the gateway, with attribution metrics."},{"title":"Rehearse","md":"Fail a region on purpose. Check quota, model versions, cold-cache cost and degraded mode."},{"title":"Review","md":"Compare cost per successful task and SLO burn monthly, then resize."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"How this round is scored","md":"Treat cost and availability as one design. Say that cheaper routing can split your cache, that failover starts cold, and that a quota is not capacity. Name the SLO before you name the product, and say which dial would flip each choice."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-l5-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 99.9% availability SLO is measured over a 30-day month. How many minutes of unavailability does the error budget allow?","answer":43.2,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"minutes","hint":"30 x 24 x 60 x 0.001","explain":"A 30-day month has 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that is 43.2 minutes. A single 40-minute incident therefore consumes about 93% of the monthly budget, which is why recovery tier and failover rehearsal matter.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r15"},{"id":"w16-l5-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each regional recovery strategy to its approximate recovery profile.","pairs":[{"l":"Hot/hot","r":"Recovery in minutes, near-zero data loss, highest cost"},{"l":"Hot/warm","r":"Recovery in 30 minutes to 2 hours, moderate cost"},{"l":"Hot/cold","r":"Recovery in 2 to 8 hours, lowest cost"}],"explain":"The Foundry guidance gives these approximate targets and says Foundry does not fail over on its own. The tier is a business choice: how much downtime and standby cost the service can accept.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r15"},{"id":"w16-l5-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After traffic failed over to a standby region, spend per request rose about 60% and p95 TTFT doubled. Traffic and prompts are unchanged, and the cached-token ratio fell to near zero. Which cause fits best?","options":["The standby region runs a larger model by default and bills at a higher rate","Prompt caches are not shared across regions, so the standby started cold","The gateway token limit counts every token twice after a failover","Priority processing was disabled by the failover and is billed at a premium"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cached-token ratio collapsing points to a cold cache: provider caches are per region and not shared across regional boundaries. A larger default model would not change the cache ratio. Double counting would cause 429s rather than higher cost per request. Priority processing does not switch off because of a failover.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r15"},{"id":"w16-l5-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Region A fails in a hot/warm setup, and region B has about 30% of normal quota. What is the best first move?","options":["Shed or degrade non-critical traffic, send critical traffic to region B, queue async work","Retry all traffic against region B until its quota resets, adding jitter between each attempt","Raise client timeouts so queued requests can wait patiently for the capacity to appear in B","Hedge every request across both regions so the first reply wins and latency stays low overall"],"answer":0,"explain":"With 30% of normal capacity, something has to give, and a product-level decision about what to drop is the only fix. Retries against exhausted quota cannot succeed. Longer timeouts only hide the queue. Hedging doubles load on the scarce region.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r1"}],"defend":{"prompt":"Token spend doubled last quarter, and last week a regional incident took our assistant offline for 40 minutes. Keep it within budget and available. Where do you start?","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["What is the traffic shape: steady, spiky, or business-hours, and what share is interactive versus asynchronous?","Where do the tokens go today, by consumer and by prompt section, and what is the cost per successful task?","What are the residency limits, and can prompts be processed outside our geography?","What are the availability target, RTO and RPO, and what degraded mode would users accept?","What capacity and quota do we have today, and what are our current 429 and cache hit rates?"],"options":[{"name":"Cut tokens first: caching, routing, output caps","when":"Spend has a clear sink such as a long stable prefix, mixed difficulty or long outputs.","tradeoff":"Routing splits the cache across models. Compression and caps risk quality. Needs an eval set."},{"name":"Gateway with per-consumer token limits, metrics and quotas","when":"Several consumers share capacity and nobody owns the bill.","tradeoff":"An extra hop and a single point of failure. Counters are per gateway and limits can briefly overshoot."},{"name":"Capacity mix: PTU baseline, spillover, Priority for bursts, Batch for async","when":"Predictable baseline with bursts and a share of delay-tolerant work.","tradeoff":"PTU is billed while idle, and quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity."},{"name":"Second region: hot/warm or hot/hot behind Front Door and APIM","when":"The availability target exceeds what one region can deliver.","tradeoff":"Standby cost, per-region quota, cold caches and state that is per region."},{"name":"Degradation plan: smaller model, shorter answers, cached answers, queue or shed","when":"Capacity can be exhausted and some work is non-critical.","tradeoff":"Needs a product decision on what to drop, and degraded answers can break guarantees."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would first ask for the traffic shape and the interactive versus asynchronous split, where tokens go by consumer and prompt section, the residency limits, the availability target with RTO and RPO, and the current quota and 429 rates. Without a cost per successful task and an SLO I would be optimizing blind."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options.** The boring options come first: output caps, a stable cached prefix and Batch for work nobody waits for. Then a gateway with per-consumer limits and metrics, a capacity mix, a second region and a degradation plan. I would treat cost and availability as one design because each lever moves the other: routing splits the cache, failover starts cold and a retry layer can multiply spend."},{"t":"p","md":"**Pick, and what flips it.** I would put a gateway in front first, with `llm-token-limit` per consumer and token metrics for attribution, because without attribution nobody owns the spend. Then I would cut tokens at the biggest sink: caching for a stable prefix, output caps for long answers, Batch for async. For capacity I would size PTUs for the steady baseline with the measured cache rate, spill over to standard for bursts and use Priority only for bursty interactive traffic. For availability I would build a hot/warm second region behind Front Door and APIM, with its own quota and deployments, and a written degraded mode."},{"t":"table","head":["If this dial changes","The choice changes to"],"rows":[["Traffic is small or spiky","Pay-as-you-go and Priority instead of PTU"],["Residency forbids global processing","Data Zone or Regional deployment types and a second region inside the boundary"],["Availability target is minutes, not hours","Hot/hot with warmed caches and tested failover"],["Spend is dominated by long unique context","Compression and retrieval changes before a router"],["Quality bar is strict","No router or smaller model without an eval gate"],["Most work is delay-tolerant","Batch first, Flex only where 429 and 408 are acceptable"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Failure modes and detection.** Retry storms from nested layers, detected by requests per unique task id. A 429 below quota, detected by comparing `x-ratelimit-limit-tokens` with the assignment. Reservations bought before capacity exists. Failover into a region without quota, or with a cold cache, found by rehearsing it. Metric dimensions with high cardinality that silently drop data. A tripped breaker that looks like a model outage, found by tagging gateway-originated 503s."},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** I would track cost per successful task, tokens per task, cache hit ratio, 429 and 503 rates, breaker-open time, TTFT and TTLT p95 by output bucket, PTU utilization and error budget burn. I would roll out limits in monitor-only mode first, shadow the second region, run a game day that fails a region on purpose, and set rollback thresholds before the first change."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about traffic shape, interactive versus async split, residency, RTO and RPO and where tokens go","w":3},{"pt":"Uses cost per successful task and attributes spend by consumer before choosing levers","w":2},{"pt":"Offers several options including the boring ones: caps, a cached prefix, Batch","w":2},{"pt":"Sizes capacity from the formula and says quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses a resilience pattern per failure type and avoids retrying quota errors","w":3},{"pt":"Plans the second region with quota, deployments, cold caches and a rehearsal","w":2},{"pt":"States flip conditions tied to dials such as traffic shape, residency and quality bar","w":2},{"pt":"Names SLIs, SLOs, detection signals and a staged rollout with rollback thresholds","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"Finance wants one global token cap for the whole company. What do you say?","a":"Use per-consumer limits and quotas at the gateway for attribution. Counters are per gateway and limits can briefly overshoot, so keep headroom below the provider limit and avoid one global number across regions."},{"q":"A 429 storm hits while your PTU usage is only 40%. Where do you look?","a":"Check which kind of 429 it is. Look at the Azure headers, compare the effective limit with the assignment, and check whether `max_tokens` inflates the counted tokens. Check spillover configuration and whether a different deployment is the one throttled."},{"q":"Security wants prompts to stay in one geography. How does that change the design?","a":"Use Data Zone or Regional deployment types and put the second region inside the same boundary. Global Standard may process prompts in any region, and a cold standby cache plus smaller quota must be planned inside the boundary."}],"redFlags":["Retries 429 quota errors or stacks retry layers without caps","Buys a reservation or PTU before confirming capacity and utilization","Proposes failover without testing it or without quota in the second region","Optimizes cost per token and never mentions cost per successful task"]},"refs":["r15","r1","r2","r3","r6","r7","r27"],"takeaways":["Cost and availability are one design: routing splits caches, failover starts cold and retries multiply spend","Attribute spend at the gateway, then cut the biggest sink first","Size PTUs for the baseline, spill over for bursts and use Batch for async work","Failover is not automatic on Foundry. Build, size and rehearse the second region","Write SLIs and an error budget, and treat a 40-minute outage as 93% of a monthly 99.9% budget"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w16-t01","order":1,"group":"Cost","title":"Where the money goes: token economics","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A model bill is the sum of uncached input, cached input, cache writes, output and reasoning tokens, multiplied by the number of calls a task makes. Knowing which sink dominates tells you which lever to pull.","useIf":"The bill grew faster than traffic, or you must price a new feature before you build it.","avoidIf":"You treat it as a one-number exercise: cost per token alone hides retries, loops and failed tasks.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Every request pays for several token categories. **Uncached input** is full price. **Cached input** is a read of reused prefix state: 0.1x the uncached rate on OpenAI GPT-5.6 and later, and 0.05x on GPT-6.1 Sol [[r25]]. A **cache write** costs 1.25x on those models, and OpenAI bills each token at exactly one of the uncached, cached or write rates. One write plus nine reads costs 2.15x against 10x uncached [[r25]]. Anthropic charges 1.25x for a 5-minute write, 2x for a 1-hour write and 0.1x for a read [[r26]]. **Output** tokens come last, and **reasoning tokens** sit inside the completion count, so a short visible answer can hide a long generation [[r27]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The visible prompt is rarely the biggest sink. **Tool definitions**, the **system prompt** and the **history** are re-sent on every call. An **agent loop** multiplies all of it by the number of model calls per task. With a constant 500 new tokens per turn, 20 turns process 500 x (1 + 2 + ... + 20) = 105,000 input tokens for 10,000 tokens of new content (derived). Caching and compaction exist to flatten that curve."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t01-d1","title":"Where tokens go in one agent step","alt":"Tool definitions, system prompt, history and retrieved context form the prompt. The model call returns reasoning and answer tokens, and the next step re-sends the grown prompt.","src":"flowchart LR\n  T[Tool definitions] --> P[Prompt]\n  S[System prompt] --> P\n  H[History] --> P\n  C[Retrieved context] --> P\n  P --> M[Model call]\n  M --> R[Reasoning tokens]\n  M --> A[Answer tokens]\n  A --> H"},{"t":"formula","title":"Cost per successful task","expr":"cost per task = sum over calls (uncached x p_in + cached x 0.1 x p_in + written x 1.25 x p_in + output x p_out) / task success rate","vars":[{"k":"p_in, p_out","v":"your price sheet per input and output token (not quoted here, prices change)"},{"k":"0.1, 1.25","v":"read and write multipliers on GPT-5.6 and later [[r25]]"},{"k":"success rate","v":"share of tasks that finish correctly; retries and failures raise cost per task"}],"example":"Five requests share one prefix inside its lifetime. Cost in units of one uncached prefix: 1.25 + 4 x 0.1 = 1.65, against 5.0 uncached (derived)."}],"whereToUse":["A finance review of a bill that grew faster than traffic","Pricing a new assistant or agent feature before launch","Choosing the first cost lever: batch, cache, trim, route or limit output","Per-tenant or per-team chargeback, using gateway token metrics [[r3]]","Comparing two models by cost per successful task, not price per million tokens"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Spend is rising and nobody can say which consumer or prompt section drives it","You are about to switch models or add caching and need a baseline","Agents or tools make several model calls per user task","You need chargeback by team, tenant or feature"],"avoid":["You treat it as a one-number exercise: cost per token alone hides retries, loops and failed tasks","Optimising a workload whose monthly spend is small against engineering time","Cutting tokens on a path where quality is not yet measured"],"flips":["Reasoning-heavy workload: output and reasoning tokens dominate, so measure completion tokens first","Long static prefix with high reuse: cached reads dominate and the write premium matters","Mostly asynchronous work: batch pricing changes the baseline by half"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to measure cost, compared","head":["Metric","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Cost per token","Comparing price sheets for the same task","Models differ in verbosity, retries or steps","A cheaper token can mean a dearer task"],["Cost per request","Single-call endpoints with stable prompts","Agents with variable call counts","Hides loops and tool fan-out"],["Cost per successful task (this topic)","Agents, retries, mixed quality","No success signal exists yet","Needs an eval or outcome label"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The bill doubled while traffic grew 20%","cause":"Longer prompts, growing history, more agent steps or a switch to a reasoning model, not more users","fix":"Attribute tokens by consumer and by prompt section, then compare tokens per task before and after"},{"symptom":"Caching was enabled and cost went up","cause":"On GPT-5.6 and later a write costs 1.25x, so a prefix used once loses 25% [[r25]]","fix":"Cache only prefixes reused at least twice inside the lifetime, or use explicit mode with no breakpoints"},{"symptom":"A cheaper model raised cost per task","cause":"More retries, longer outputs or extra agent steps offset the lower price","fix":"Track cost per successful task and tokens per task, not price per token"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t01-s1","title":"The bill doubled, traffic did not","situation":"A support assistant answers questions with retrieval. Monthly model spend doubled while requests grew 20%. Nobody owns the prompt, and several teams call one shared deployment.","constraints":["Quality must not drop","Prices and model mix are unknown","One shared deployment serves several teams"],"unknowns":["Did the average prompt or the average output grow?","Which team or feature grew fastest?","Did anyone change the model, effort level or retrieval depth?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Attribute tokens by consumer and prompt section, then pick the biggest sink","verdict":"best","why":"Without attribution any lever is a guess. A gateway metric by team and a split of prompt tokens into system, tools, history and retrieval show where growth came from.","tradeoff":"Costs a day or two of instrumentation before any saving lands."},{"name":"Swap to the cheapest model for every request","verdict":"poor","why":"It cuts price per token but may raise retries, output length and escalations, and it changes quality unmeasured.","tradeoff":"Right only after evals show the cheap model passes the quality bar on this task mix."},{"name":"Lower max_tokens everywhere","verdict":"good","why":"Output is the costliest token class and caps are cheap to ship.","tradeoff":"Truncated answers hurt quality if the cap is below natural answer length; it does not touch input growth."}],"flips":[{"if":"Attribution shows 70% of tokens are an unchanged 9,000-token prefix","then":"Fix prefix ordering and caching first."},{"if":"One batch-like consumer grew fastest and nobody waits for it","then":"Move that consumer to Batch for half price."}],"takeaway":"Attribute first. The lever follows the sink."},{"id":"w16-t01-s2","title":"A cheap-looking classifier","situation":"A classification endpoint runs 5 million short requests a day on a reasoning model. Visible answers are one word. Cost is far above the estimate from the price sheet.","constraints":["Task has labelled examples","Latency budget is 1 second","Volume is high and steady"],"unknowns":["How many reasoning tokens does each call use?","What accuracy does a non-reasoning model reach on the labelled set?","Is the effort level configurable?"],"question":"Where is the money going and what is the fix?","options":[{"name":"Check completion token details for reasoning tokens, then lower effort or use a non-reasoning model if evals hold","verdict":"best","why":"Reasoning tokens count inside completion tokens, so a one-word answer can cost thousands of hidden tokens.","tradeoff":"Accuracy must be re-measured on the labelled set before the switch."},{"name":"Add a semantic cache","verdict":"good","why":"Repeated short inputs can hit, saving whole calls.","tradeoff":"Adds an embedding and lookup per miss, and wrong hits are a quality risk."},{"name":"Buy PTUs for the volume","verdict":"poor","why":"It changes how you pay, not how many tokens each call burns.","tradeoff":"Right after tokens per call are minimised and traffic is steady."}],"flips":[{"if":"Nobody waits for the result","then":"Batch the work for half price."},{"if":"Accuracy drops sharply without reasoning","then":"Keep reasoning on hard cases only, behind a router."}],"takeaway":"Hidden reasoning tokens turn a one-word answer into a large bill."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t01-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A prefix is written once at 1.25x and then read 4 more times at 0.1x inside its lifetime. What is the total cost, in units of one uncached pass over the prefix?","answer":1.65,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"x","hint":"1.25 plus four reads","explain":"1.25 + 4 x 0.1 = 1.65. Five uncached passes would cost 5.0. The same arithmetic on one write plus nine reads gives the 2.15x in the OpenAI guide [[r25]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w16-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"A user sends one message and the agent makes 10 model calls. Which of these are paid as input tokens on each of the 10 calls?","options":["Tool definitions","System prompt","Only the user message","Earlier tool results kept in history"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Tool definitions, the system prompt and the accumulated history are re-sent every call. The user message is not the only input; it is one small part. Caching lowers the price of the repeated parts but does not remove them.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w16-t01-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Cost per token fell 40% after a model switch, but cost per task rose. Which cause is most consistent?","options":["Cached reads cost 0.1x","The new model needs more steps and retries per task","Batch pricing applied by mistake"],"answer":1,"explain":"More steps and longer outputs raise tokens per task faster than price per token fell. Cached reads and batch pricing both lower cost, so neither explains a rise.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"On GPT-5.6 and later, what do cache writes and reads cost relative to uncached input?","back":"Write 1.25x, read 0.1x (0.05x on GPT-6.1 Sol). One write plus nine reads is 2.15x against 10x uncached."},{"front":"Why can a one-word answer be expensive?","back":"Reasoning tokens count inside completion tokens. The hidden generation is billed even when the visible answer is short."},{"front":"Which metric hides retries and agent loops?","back":"Cost per token. Track cost per successful task and tokens per task instead."}],"related":["w16-t02","w16-t03","w16-t06","w16-l1","w02-t11"],"refs":["r25","r26","r27","r3"]},{"id":"w16-t02","order":2,"group":"Cost","title":"Cost levers","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Six levers cut spend: trim the prompt, cache the stable prefix, cap output, choose a cheaper model, move work to batch tiers, and reuse whole answers. Each has a break-even and a catch.","useIf":"You know which token sink dominates and must pick the lever that fits it.","avoidIf":"You have not attributed spend yet, or you would trade answer quality you have not measured.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Pull levers in the order of **sink size, effort and risk**. Output tokens are the costliest class, and OpenAI's rule of thumb is that halving output tokens may cut about half the latency while halving the prompt may cut only 1% to 5% [[tr8]]. Input trimming still saves money in proportion to tokens, so latency and cost do not rank levers the same way."},{"t":"p","md":"**Prompt caching** reads a repeated prefix at 0.1x but writes it at 1.25x on GPT-5.6 and later, so it breaks even at the second request inside the lifetime: 1.25 + 0.1(N-1) against N [[r25]]. Anthropic's 1-hour entry writes at 2x and needs three requests (2.2x against 3x) [[r26]]. A **semantic cache** skips the whole call on a hit but pays an embedding and a vector lookup on every miss, and Microsoft warns hits can be incorrect, outdated or unsafe [[tr1]]. **Batch** and **Flex** halve the price in return for time [[r12]] [[r11]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"The levers in one view","head":["Lever","What it cuts","Evidence","Catch"],"rows":[["Trim prompt","Input tokens","Cost falls in proportion; latency barely moves [[tr8]]","Dropped context lowers answer quality"],["Prompt cache","Input price of a reused prefix","Read 0.1x, write 1.25x [[r25]]","Silent misses when the prefix drifts"],["Cap output","Output tokens","Azure advises lower max_tokens and stop sequences [[r27]]","Truncated answers"],["Cheaper or routed model","Price per token","Cascades matched the best model with up to 98% lower cost in 2023 tests [[r20]]","Needs evals; splits the cache"],["Batch or Flex","Price per token","50% less [[r12]] [[r11]]","24-hour target or no SLA"],["Semantic cache","Whole calls","Policy pair lookup and store [[tr1]]","Wrong, stale or leaked answers"],["Prompt compression","Input tokens","LLMLingua up to 20x with little loss on tested sets [[r22]]","Exact quotations can break"]]}],"whereToUse":["A cost sprint after attribution shows the biggest token sink","Designing a prompt so the stable part comes first and caches","Deciding which workloads move to Batch or Flex","Setting max_tokens and stop sequences per endpoint","Tuning an APIM semantic cache for a public FAQ"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Input is dominated by a long, stable prefix: cache it","Nobody waits for the result: batch it","Many queries are paraphrases of each other and low risk: semantic cache","Outputs are longer than users need: cap them"],"avoid":["Caching a prefix reused once per lifetime: the write premium loses","Semantic caching answers built from permissioned or fast-changing data","Compressing prompts that contain quotations the answer must reproduce"],"flips":["Gaps between requests exceed the lifetime: a longer TTL or prewarm, or skip caching","Quality evals fail on the cheaper model: keep the large model and cut elsewhere","Answers depend on the user's permissions: scope or skip the semantic cache"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Choosing between levers","head":["Lever","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt cache","Long stable prefix, steady traffic","Prefix changes every call, one use per lifetime","Write at 1.25x, hit rate must be monitored"],["Semantic cache","High paraphrase rate, public, low-risk content","Permissioned data, fast-changing facts, regulated answers","Wrong or leaked answers, extra lookup on misses"],["Batch or Flex","Delay-tolerant work","Interactive requests","No real-time SLA; Flex can return 429 or 408"],["Routing to a smaller model","Wide difficulty spread, trusted evals","Uniform hard tasks","Quality regressions, split prompt cache"],["Trimming and capping","Verbose prompts or answers","Context really is needed","Quality loss that is hard to see"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Cache hit ratio is near zero after a release","cause":"A timestamp, reordered tools or a changed effort setting moved the prefix","fix":"Put static content first, keep tool order stable, alert on cached tokens over prompt tokens"},{"symptom":"A semantic cache returns another user's answer","cause":"The cache key ignores tenant, user, model or prompt version","fix":"Partition with `vary-by` and include model, embedding model, template and policy versions in the key [[tr2]]"},{"symptom":"Semantic cache saves nothing","cause":"Low repeat rate, so most requests pay embedding plus lookup plus the normal call","fix":"Measure net savings per miss and hit; drop the cache if hits stay low"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t02-s1","title":"A fat, stable prefix in an agent loop","situation":"A tool-using agent makes 8 to 15 calls per task. Each call carries 12,000 tokens of tool definitions and system text plus a growing history.","constraints":["The tool set is stable inside a session","Tasks are interactive","Traffic is steady through the day"],"unknowns":["Is the prefix byte-identical across calls?","How often do sessions go idle longer than the lifetime?","Is the deployment PTU-M, which has no breakpoints?"],"question":"Which change saves the most first?","options":[{"name":"Order tools and system first, keep history append-only, remove early timestamps","verdict":"best","why":"Calls after the first read the 12,000 tokens at a tenth of the price and with lower time to first token.","tradeoff":"Any tool change or compaction resets reuse, and each first write costs 1.25x."},{"name":"Trim tool results and compact old history","verdict":"good","why":"Shrinks the growing part of the prompt.","tradeoff":"Compaction rewrites the prefix and resets the cache."},{"name":"Move every step to the smallest model","verdict":"poor","why":"Weaker planning can add steps, which multiplies the re-sent prefix.","tradeoff":"Fine for narrow sub-steps behind a router once evals pass."}],"flips":[{"if":"Sessions rarely make a second call inside the lifetime","then":"Caching saves little; shrink the tool list instead."},{"if":"Gaps between calls run 5 to 60 minutes on Anthropic","then":"The 1-hour TTL can pay, but only from the third request."}],"takeaway":"In loops the repeated prefix is the bill. Make it cacheable first."},{"id":"w16-t02-s2","title":"Nightly enrichment of two million records","situation":"A pipeline classifies and summarises records overnight. Results are read the next morning. Volume is large and the model is fixed.","constraints":["Nobody waits for individual results","Window is eight hours","Volume repeats every night"],"unknowns":["Does the model support the batch tier?","Is enqueued-token quota enough for one night's input?","How are partial failures retried?"],"question":"Where does the work run?","options":[{"name":"Batch tier with a 24-hour target","verdict":"best","why":"Half price on Azure, OpenAI and Anthropic, with separate quota that does not touch interactive traffic.","tradeoff":"No real-time SLA, so the eight-hour window is a risk if jobs queue longer; split jobs and monitor."},{"name":"Flex processing","verdict":"good","why":"Also 50% off with a synchronous API shape.","tradeoff":"Preview on Azure, preemptible: expect 429 and 408 and build your own retry. It shares quota with Standard."},{"name":"Provisioned throughput for the nightly peak","verdict":"poor","why":"PTUs bill every hour whether or not requests arrive, so a nightly job pays for idle days.","tradeoff":"Right for steady, latency-sensitive volume."}],"flips":[{"if":"Results must be ready within 30 minutes","then":"Batch is too slow; use Flex with fallback or Standard."},{"if":"The model has no batch support","then":"Use Standard with a client-side rate limiter and off-peak scheduling."}],"takeaway":"If nobody waits, do not pay for waiting."},{"id":"w16-t02-s3","title":"A public FAQ assistant","situation":"A public assistant answers product questions from one shared knowledge base. About 40% of questions are paraphrases of the same few hundred. No user data is involved.","constraints":["Content is identical for every caller","A wrong answer is a minor support cost","Answers change when the knowledge base is republished"],"unknowns":["What is the measured paraphrase rate?","How strict can the similarity threshold be?","How is the cache invalidated on republish?"],"question":"Is a semantic cache worth it?","options":[{"name":"Yes: gateway semantic cache, strict threshold, TTL, flush on republish","verdict":"best","why":"Content is shared and low risk, so a hit saves the whole call. Start near 0.05 on the APIM scale and add the model and prompt version to the partition.","tradeoff":"Every miss pays embedding plus lookup; wrong hits are possible."},{"name":"Exact-match response cache only","verdict":"good","why":"No similarity risk and simple invalidation.","tradeoff":"Misses paraphrases, so hit rate is lower."},{"name":"No cache; rely on prompt caching","verdict":"poor","why":"It leaves 40% repeat traffic paying full output cost.","tradeoff":"Right when answers are permissioned or the repeat rate is low."}],"flips":[{"if":"Answers depend on the caller's entitlements","then":"Partition by identity scope or skip semantic caching."},{"if":"Repeat rate measures under 10%","then":"Net savings are small; skip it."}],"takeaway":"Semantic caching pays for shared, low-risk, repetitive content only."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t02-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"An Anthropic 1-hour cache entry writes at 2x and reads at 0.1x. What is the smallest number of requests inside the hour for which caching costs less than uncached?","answer":3,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"requests","explain":"Cost is 2 + 0.1(N-1) against N. N=2 gives 2.1 against 2, which loses. N=3 gives 2.2 against 3, which wins [[r26]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"You deploy an APIM semantic cache for the first time. Which `score-threshold` is the safest start?","options":["0.8, because higher means more hits","0.5, the midpoint","About 0.05, then tune with measured wrong-hit rates"],"answer":2,"explain":"In APIM a lower score is stricter. Microsoft suggests starting low and warns that above 0.2 may mismatch [[tr1]]. Higher values add hits and wrong answers; 0.8 would match loosely.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t02-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each lever to its main catch.","pairs":[{"l":"Prompt cache","r":"Silent misses when the prefix drifts"},{"l":"Semantic cache","r":"Wrong, stale or leaked answers"},{"l":"Batch","r":"24-hour target, no real-time SLA"},{"l":"Prompt compression","r":"Exact quotations can break"}],"explain":"Each lever fails differently. The prompt cache keeps the model output unchanged but needs monitoring; a semantic cache changes what users see.","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When does a 1.25x write plus 0.1x reads beat uncached?","back":"From the second request inside the lifetime: 1.25 + 0.1(N-1) against N. One use loses 25%."},{"front":"Which saves more latency: halving output or halving input?","back":"Output, roughly half the latency by OpenAI's rule of thumb. Halving input may save only 1% to 5% of latency, though it still cuts cost."},{"front":"What does a semantic cache cost on a miss?","back":"An embedding call plus a vector lookup before the normal model call. Net savings need a high hit rate."}],"related":["w16-t01","w16-t03","w16-t06","w02-t11","w02-t15","w16-l1"],"refs":["r25","r26","r27","r12","r11","r20","r22","tr1","tr2","tr8"]},{"id":"w16-t03","order":3,"group":"Cost","title":"Routing and cascades as a cost lever","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A router picks a model per request, and a cascade tries a cheap model first and escalates when a check fails. Both save money only when query difficulty varies and quality is measured.","useIf":"Most queries are easy, a few are hard, and you have an eval set that can tell them apart.","avoidIf":"Tasks are uniformly hard, no eval set exists, or the prompt cache is your main saving.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **router** decides before the call which model should answer. A **cascade** calls a cheap model, checks the result and escalates only on failure. FrugalGPT reported a cascade that matched the best single model with up to 98% lower cost, or beat GPT-4 by 4% at equal cost, on 2023 benchmarks and prices [[r20]]. RouteLLM trained routers on preference data and cut cost by more than 2x in some settings with no quality loss [[r21]]. Treat these as proof the idea works, not as your savings."},{"t":"p","md":"Azure's **model router** is one deployment that routes per request across OpenAI, DeepSeek, Meta, xAI and Anthropic models. Modes are `Balanced` (the default, cheapest within about 1% to 2% of the best quality), `Cost` (about 5% to 6%) and `Quality`. Version `2025-11-18` is current and updates in place, while `2025-08-07` and `2025-05-19` are frozen. It runs on Global Standard and Data Zone Standard, reads text only, and its context window equals the smallest model in the pool [[r14]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Cascade break-even","expr":"cascade cost = c_cheap + c_check + p_escalate x c_strong; it wins when p_escalate < 1 - (c_cheap + c_check) / c_strong","vars":[{"k":"c_cheap, c_strong","v":"cost of one call on each model"},{"k":"c_check","v":"cost of the verifier or validator"},{"k":"p_escalate","v":"share of queries the check rejects"}],"example":"c_strong = 10, c_cheap = 1, c_check = 0.5, p_escalate = 0.3: cost = 1.5 + 3 = 4.5, a 55% saving. Escalated queries also pay both latencies (derived)."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t03-d1","title":"A cascade with a verifier","alt":"A query goes to a cheap model, a check accepts or rejects, rejected queries go to the strong model.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Query] --> C[Cheap model]\n  C --> V{Check passes}\n  V -->|yes| A[Answer]\n  V -->|no| S[Strong model]\n  S --> A"}],"whereToUse":["A support or search assistant with a wide spread of query difficulty","Extraction pipelines where a schema or rule check can verify a cheap model's output","One endpoint for many teams, using the Azure model router with a model subset","Classifying intent first, then sending only complex intents to the large model"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A good share of traffic is easy and a smaller model passes your eval on it","A reliable verifier exists, such as schema validation, tests or retrieval overlap","Latency of an extra hop on hard queries is acceptable"],"avoid":["The same model is cheap enough already and engineering time costs more","Quality evals do not cover the hard tail","Residency limits the pool so far that routing has little to choose from"],"flips":["High escalation rate: cost approaches the strong model plus overhead, so use one model","Long shared prefix: routing across models splits the prompt cache, so measure both together","Context windows differ: the router uses the smallest model's window, so long prompts need a pinned subset"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Routing options compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Single model","Small team, uniform tasks","Easy traffic pays the large-model price","Highest unit cost"],["Static rules by task type","Clear task types, stable models","Rules drift as models change","Manual upkeep"],["Trained router (Azure model router)","Mixed difficulty, one endpoint wanted","Pool changes silently, text-only routing","Cache split, smallest context window [[r14]]"],["Cascade with verifier","Cheap model right most of the time, verifier reliable","Many escalations, weak verifier","Double spend and latency on hard queries"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Quality dips with no deployment change","cause":"The router version `2025-11-18` updates in place, so the model pool changed without a version bump [[r14]]","fix":"Pin a model subset, keep a regression eval and a shadow comparison"},{"symptom":"Cache hit ratio fell after adding a router","cause":"Consecutive requests reach different models, and each model has its own cache","fix":"Measure cost with caching on both sides; use session affinity where offered, noting it is preview and no guarantee for Chat Completions"},{"symptom":"Escalations are common and cost went up","cause":"The verifier is weak or the cheap model fails the hard tail","fix":"Track escalation rate, tighten routing features, or drop the cascade"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t03-s1","title":"A mixed-difficulty support assistant","situation":"An assistant handles thousands of questions a day. Most are simple look-ups, some need multi-step reasoning. A labelled eval set exists. Data may be processed globally.","constraints":["Latency p95 target of 3 seconds","Global processing is allowed","An eval set with difficulty labels exists"],"unknowns":["What share of queries is easy?","Does a long shared prefix make caching the bigger lever?","Who owns regressions after a router update?"],"question":"How do you cut cost without hurting quality?","options":[{"name":"Azure model router in Balanced mode with a pinned subset, shadow evals and rollback","verdict":"best","why":"One endpoint, per-request choice, and Balanced stays within about 1% to 2% of best quality by Microsoft's description.","tradeoff":"Caching is split across models and the in-place version update needs monitoring."},{"name":"Static rules by intent label","verdict":"good","why":"Predictable and cheap to run.","tradeoff":"Rules go stale as models change and misroute hard queries."},{"name":"Cascade where the cheap model grades its own answer","verdict":"poor","why":"Self-grading is a weak verifier, so escalations or missed errors rise.","tradeoff":"Right when a deterministic check exists, such as schema or tests."}],"flips":[{"if":"A 9,000-token shared prefix dominates cost","then":"A single model with caching can beat routing."},{"if":"Prompts exceed the smallest pooled model's window","then":"Pin a subset of large-window models or route by length yourself."}],"takeaway":"Routing pays on wide difficulty spread with measured quality."},{"id":"w16-t03-s2","title":"Extraction with a hard validator","situation":"A pipeline extracts invoice fields. A schema check and a checksum validate every output. Volume is high and the cheap model is right about 85% of the time.","constraints":["Validator is deterministic","Failures can be retried on a stronger model","Batch is not possible, results feed a live form"],"unknowns":["What does an escalation cost in latency?","Do validator failures correlate with document type?"],"question":"Which design fits?","options":[{"name":"Cascade: cheap model, validator, escalate failures","verdict":"best","why":"The verifier is reliable, escalation is about 15%, so cost is roughly c_cheap + 0.15 x c_strong.","tradeoff":"The 15% of escalated requests pay two calls and two latencies."},{"name":"Trained router on document features","verdict":"good","why":"Avoids paying twice on documents known to be hard.","tradeoff":"Needs training data and misroutes unseen formats."},{"name":"Always the strong model","verdict":"poor","why":"It pays the large price for the 85% that are easy.","tradeoff":"Right when the strong model is only slightly dearer or errors are unacceptable."}],"flips":[{"if":"Escalation rises above the break-even rate","then":"Use the strong model directly."},{"if":"No validator exists","then":"Do not cascade; use a router with evals or one model."}],"takeaway":"A cascade is only as good as its verifier."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t03-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A cascade has c_cheap = 1, c_check = 0.5 and c_strong = 10 (any unit). What is the highest escalation rate at which it still beats always using the strong model? Give the rate as a percentage.","answer":85,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"%","hint":"1 - (1 + 0.5) / 10","explain":"Break-even: 1.5 + p x 10 = 10, so p = 0.85. In practice latency and quality make the real threshold lower. Derived from the formula above.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After adding the Azure model router, prompt cache hits drop. Which cause fits best?","options":["Consecutive requests reach different underlying models","The router rewrites system prompts","Routing disables all caching"],"answer":0,"explain":"Caching helps only when consecutive requests reach the same model [[r14]]. The router does not rewrite prompts, and caching is not disabled but split.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t03-q3","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which changes favour a single pinned model over a router or cascade?","options":["A long shared prefix dominates spend","Hard queries are rare and a verifier is reliable","There is no eval set that covers hard queries","Escalation rate would exceed 70%"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"A dominant prefix, missing evals and a high escalation rate each remove the router's advantage. Rare hard queries with a reliable verifier favour a cascade.","difficulty":3}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Router versus cascade?","back":"A router picks a model before the call. A cascade calls the cheap model first, checks the result and escalates on failure."},{"front":"What are the Azure model router modes?","back":"Balanced (default, within about 1% to 2% of best quality), Cost (about 5% to 6%) and Quality."},{"front":"Two hidden costs of routing?","back":"It splits the prompt cache across models, and the router's context window equals the smallest model in the pool."}],"related":["w16-t01","w16-t02","w16-t10","w16-l1","w01-t17"],"refs":["r14","r20","r21","r25"]},{"id":"w16-t04","order":4,"group":"Capacity","title":"Quotas, TPM, RPM and 429s","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Providers limit tokens per minute and requests per minute. A 429 has more than one cause, and each provider counts tokens differently, so read the headers before you raise anything.","useIf":"Requests fail with 429 while dashboards show spare quota, or many consumers share one deployment.","avoidIf":"You plan to fix every 429 by asking for more quota or by retrying harder.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"On Azure, **quota** is a TPM allowance per subscription, region, model and deployment type. The TPM you assign becomes the deployment's limit, and **RPM** follows in proportion: 6 RPM per 1,000 TPM for older chat models and 1 RPM per 1,000 TPM for o3 and o4-mini [[r8]]. Responses carry `x-ratelimit-limit-requests`, `x-ratelimit-limit-tokens`, `x-ratelimit-remaining-requests`, `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` and matching `reset` headers, and a 429 adds `retry-after-ms` [[r8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Azure documents several kinds of 429. Three to tell apart first are quota exceeded, system capacity throttling, and a temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard deployments. Compare `x-ratelimit-limit-tokens` with your assigned TPM to spot the third [[r8]]. A fourth documented cause is a request whose `max_tokens` estimate inflates the counted tokens, so a 429 can appear below quota. The TPM check uses prompt tokens plus `max_tokens` plus `best_of`, so `max_tokens: 4000` reserves 4,000 tokens for a 200-token answer, and RPM is enforced over 1 or 10 second windows, so a 600 RPM deployment can throttle at more than 10 requests in one second [[r9]]."},{"t":"p","md":"At scale, enforce **per-consumer limits at a gateway**: `llm-token-limit` limits by a `counter-key` in tokens per minute or as a quota per period, but its counts are estimates and are kept per gateway [[r2]]. When a 429 does arrive, honour `retry-after-ms` and never retry a quota-exhausted error."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t04-d1","title":"Reading a 429 (author synthesis)","alt":"A decision tree: if the limit header is below assigned TPM it is a temporary cut; if remaining headers are near zero it is quota or an RPM window; otherwise capacity throttling.","src":"flowchart TB\n  A[429 received] --> B{limit-tokens below assigned TPM}\n  B -->|yes| C[Temporary limit cut]\n  B -->|no| D{remaining near zero}\n  D -->|yes| E[Quota or short RPM window]\n  D -->|no| F[Capacity throttling]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Providers count differently","head":["Provider","Limits","Counting detail"],"rows":[["Azure OpenAI","TPM and RPM per deployment","Prompt plus `max_tokens` counted up front [[r9]]"],["OpenAI","RPM, RPD, TPM, TPD, IPM per org, project and model","Counts the larger of `max_tokens` and an estimate; 429 `slow_down`, 503 `server_is_overloaded` [[r16]]"],["Anthropic","RPM, ITPM, OTPM, token bucket with continuous refill","OTPM counts actual output only; cache reads do not count toward ITPM for most models; acceleration limits punish sharp ramps [[r17]]"]]}],"whereToUse":["Capacity planning before a launch: choose TPM and RPM per deployment","Incident triage for a spike of 429s","Setting `max_tokens` per endpoint so reservations match answers","Per-consumer limits at an API gateway with `llm-token-limit` [[r2]]","Ramping traffic against Anthropic acceleration limits"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Several teams share one deployment and need fair shares","429s appear while average usage is below quota","You budget with a provider that counts `max_tokens` up front"],"avoid":["Retrying quota-exhausted 429s in a loop","Raising quota when the cause is a temporary adjustment or capacity throttling","Leaving `max_tokens` at a large default on OpenAI or Azure"],"flips":["Sustained steady demand above quota: buy provisioned capacity or add a second deployment","Bursts above RPM windows: smooth at the gateway with a token bucket or queue","Anthropic with a high cache hit rate: cache reads free ITPM, so measure uncached input"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where to enforce limits","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Provider quota only","One consumer, small scale","Many consumers share a deployment","One noisy consumer starves the rest"],["Gateway `llm-token-limit` per consumer","Fair shares, chargeback, many teams","Counters must span regions","Estimates, brief overshoot, counters are per gateway [[r2]]"],["Separate deployments per workload","Strong isolation needed","Quota is scarce","Fragmented quota, more to manage"],["Client-side limiter","A single app, quick fix","Many clients","No central view; each client guesses"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"429s while TPM usage looks low","cause":"Large `max_tokens` reserves tokens, or a burst exceeds the 1 to 10 second RPM window","fix":"Set `max_tokens` near the real answer length and smooth bursts"},{"symptom":"Raising quota changed nothing","cause":"The 429 was capacity throttling or a temporary limit cut, not quota","fix":"Read the headers, compare limit with assigned TPM, move traffic to another deployment or region"},{"symptom":"A retry loop turned a blip into an outage","cause":"Quota-exhausted 429s were retried and each layer multiplied traffic","fix":"Honour `retry-after-ms`, do not retry quota errors, count retries across SDK and app layers"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t04-s1","title":"429s with spare quota on the dashboard","situation":"An assistant answers in about 300 tokens but every call sets `max_tokens: 4000`. At peak, calls return 429 while the TPM graph reads 55% of quota.","constraints":["Azure Standard deployment","Peak traffic is bursty","No budget for provisioned capacity"],"unknowns":["What do `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` and the limit header say?","How many requests arrive in the busiest second?","Is there an overly wide `best_of` or `n`?"],"question":"What is the first fix?","options":[{"name":"Set `max_tokens` close to the real answer length and read the headers","verdict":"best","why":"Azure's TPM check counts prompt plus `max_tokens`, so each call reserves about 13 times what it uses. Right-sizing frees most of the allowance at no cost.","tradeoff":"Answers longer than the cap are truncated, so measure the output length distribution first."},{"name":"Request more quota","verdict":"poor","why":"It treats a reservation artefact as a shortage and the request may be slow or denied.","tradeoff":"Right when remaining-tokens headers sit near zero at sustained load."},{"name":"Add client retries with backoff","verdict":"good","why":"Backoff with jitter smooths short bursts.","tradeoff":"It does not reduce reservation waste and multiplies load if every layer retries."}],"flips":[{"if":"The limit header is lower than assigned TPM","then":"It is a temporary cut: route to a second deployment and wait."},{"if":"Bursts exceed the RPM window with ample TPM","then":"Smooth at a gateway queue or token bucket."}],"takeaway":"Oversized `max_tokens` is a quota tax. Right-size it before you ask for more."},{"id":"w16-t04-s2","title":"One team starves the others","situation":"Five teams share one Azure deployment. A new batch-style job from one team consumes most of the TPM at midday, and interactive teams see 429s.","constraints":["One deployment, one quota","Teams need predictable shares","A gateway can be added"],"unknowns":["Can the batch job move to a Batch tier?","Do teams need monthly token quotas for chargeback?"],"question":"How do you protect interactive users?","options":[{"name":"Per-team `llm-token-limit` at the gateway, and move the batch job to Batch","verdict":"best","why":"Limits give each team a fair share with a 429 on rate and 403 on quota, and the Batch tier has its own enqueued-token quota.","tradeoff":"Gateway estimates tokens and can briefly overshoot; counters are per gateway."},{"name":"A separate deployment per team","verdict":"good","why":"Strong isolation.","tradeoff":"Fragments quota so each team needs headroom for its own peaks."},{"name":"Ask every team to retry more patiently","verdict":"poor","why":"Retries add load while the noisy job keeps the quota exhausted.","tradeoff":"Backoff is right for transient blips, not for a sustained shortfall."}],"flips":[{"if":"The gateway runs in several regions","then":"Counters are not aggregated, so split the limit per region or use a shared store."},{"if":"Only two teams share quota","then":"Two deployments may be simpler than a gateway."}],"takeaway":"Shared quota needs per-consumer limits, or the loudest consumer wins."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A call has a 600-token prompt, `max_tokens: 4000` and returns 400 tokens. On Azure OpenAI, how many tokens does the TPM check reserve for this call (ignore `best_of`)?","answer":4600,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","explain":"Azure counts prompt plus `max_tokens` up front, so 600 + 4000 = 4,600 [[r9]]. The 400 actual output tokens are irrelevant to the reservation.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w16-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A 429 arrives and `x-ratelimit-limit-tokens` is well below the TPM you assigned. Which cause is most likely?","options":["A malformed request body","Your quota is permanently reduced","A temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard"],"answer":2,"explain":"Azure lists a temporary adjustment as one of the documented 429 causes and tells you to compare the limit header with assigned TPM [[r8]]. A malformed body returns 400, and assigned quota is unchanged.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t04-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"You use Anthropic and set a high `max_tokens` for safety. Which statement is correct?","options":["It reserves tokens up front exactly as on Azure","It does not count toward OTPM, which counts actual output","It raises ITPM"],"answer":1,"explain":"Anthropic's OTPM counts actual output only, unlike OpenAI and Azure [[r17]]. ITPM concerns input tokens, so a larger output cap does not change it.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are Azure's three causes of 429?","back":"Quota exceeded, system capacity throttling, and a temporary rate-limit adjustment on Standard. Compare the limit header with assigned TPM."},{"front":"Why can `max_tokens: 4000` cause 429s?","back":"Azure and OpenAI count it toward the limit up front, so each call reserves 4,000 tokens whatever it generates."},{"front":"What does `llm-token-limit` return when exceeded?","back":"429 for the rate limit and 403 for the quota. Counters are per gateway, and streaming always estimates."}],"related":["w16-t05","w16-t07","w16-t08","w16-l2","w02-t01"],"refs":["r8","r9","r16","r17","r2"]},{"id":"w16-t05","order":5,"group":"Capacity","title":"Deployment types and capacity: PAYG, global, data zone, PTU","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Azure sells model capacity as pay-as-you-go Global, Data Zone or regional Standard, as Priority processing, and as provisioned throughput (PTU). The choice follows traffic shape, residency and how tightly latency must hold.","useIf":"You must pick how to buy tokens for a production workload and defend the choice with numbers.","avoidIf":"Traffic is unknown. Start on Global Standard, measure, then size commitments.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry offers `GlobalStandard`, `DataZoneStandard`, `Standard` (a single geography), the provisioned types `GlobalProvisionedManaged`, `DataZoneProvisionedManaged` and `ProvisionedManaged`, the batch types `GlobalBatch` and `DataZoneBatch`, and `DeveloperTier`. Microsoft advises starting with Global Standard. Global may process in any region, Data Zone stays inside the US, EU or APAC zone, and Standard stays in one geography; data at rest stays in the designated geography. Global and Data Zone Standard are not a failover: if the primary region has an interruption, all traffic initially routed to it is affected, so neither replaces a second region [[r5]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**PTU** capacity is held whether or not requests arrive. It is billed per PTU per hour or through 1-month or 1-year reservations. Quota is per subscription, region and deployment type, and **quota does not guarantee capacity**; neither do reservations, so create the deployment first. Cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity [[r6]]. **Priority processing** is a pay-as-you-go tier set per deployment or with `service_tier: \"priority\"` per request. Its target is a p50 token rate per 5-minute window, not a per-request guarantee [[r10]]. **Spillover** sends non-200 overflow from a full PTU deployment to a standard one in the same resource, for all requests or per request through `x-ms-spillover-deployment` [[r7]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"PTU sizing","expr":"normalized TPM = peak RPM x prompt tokens x (1 - cache rate) + output-to-input ratio x peak RPM x response tokens; PTUs = normalized TPM / input TPM per PTU, rounded up to the model increment","vars":[{"k":"input TPM per PTU","v":"model specific, from the sizing doc"},{"k":"increment","v":"smallest step of PTUs for that model"}],"example":"Doc example: 1,000 RPM, 200-token prompt, 20-token response, ratio 8, 3,400 input TPM per PTU, increment 5. 360,000 / 3,400 = 105.88, so 110 PTUs. At a 50% cache rate it is 260,000 / 3,400 = 76.47, so 80 PTUs [[r7]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Capacity options at a glance","head":["Option","Processing boundary","Wins when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Global Standard","Any region","Default, newest models, highest quota","Residency limits; variance at large steady volume"],["Data Zone Standard","US, EU or APAC zone","Zone-level residency","Newest models arrive later"],["Priority processing","Global or Data Zone Standard (US)","Bursty, latency-sensitive, no commitment","Price premium; p50 target only"],["Provisioned (PTU) with spillover","Global, Data Zone or regional","Steady, high volume, stable latency","Spiky or small traffic; idle cost; capacity not guaranteed"]]}],"whereToUse":["Launch planning: Global Standard first, then measure peak RPM and token mix","EU-resident workloads: Data Zone Standard or Data Zone Provisioned","Business-hour apps with bursts: Priority processing instead of a commitment","Steady high-volume APIs with p95 targets: PTU plus spillover","Cost reviews: compare reservations with measured utilization"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Provisioned: traffic is predictable and high enough to keep PTUs busy","Priority: latency matters but traffic is bursty","Data Zone: a residency boundary applies","Spillover: provisioned baseline with occasional peaks"],"avoid":["PTUs for small or spiky traffic: capacity bills while idle","Assuming a quota or reservation gives capacity in a region","Spillover for Azure DeepSeek or Meta Llama, which are not supported"],"flips":["A high prompt cache rate lowers PTU count, so redesign prefixes before sizing","A new residency rule moves you from Global to Data Zone and may delay model availability","Traffic becomes steady 24 hours a day: reservations beat hourly PTU billing at high utilization"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Pay-as-you-go versus provisioned","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Global Standard","Unknown or variable traffic","Strict residency, tight latency variance","Shared capacity, 429s at peaks"],["Data Zone Standard","Zone residency needed","Latest model on day one","Smaller pool than Global"],["Priority","Bursts with latency targets","You need a hard SLA","Premium price, p50 per 5 minutes only [[r10]]"],["PTU with spillover","Steady volume, stable latency","Spiky traffic, small scale","Idle cost; scale-up capacity can be unavailable [[r6]]"],["Batch or Flex","Delay-tolerant work","Interactive","No real-time SLA (see the batch topic)"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A PTU deployment cannot be created despite a reservation","cause":"Quota and reservations do not guarantee regional capacity","fix":"Create the deployment first, then buy the reservation; keep a second region in mind"},{"symptom":"PTU bill is high and utilization low","cause":"Sized for peak on spiky traffic","fix":"Size PTUs for the baseline, spill the peaks over to Standard, and use Batch for async work"},{"symptom":"Cache breakpoints have no effect on a PTU-M deployment","cause":"PTU-M keeps caching but has no breakpoints and no `cache_write_tokens`","fix":"Order the prompt for implicit reuse; count cached tokens as free capacity"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t05-s1","title":"Steady API inside an EU boundary","situation":"An internal API serves a steady 800 requests per minute around the clock. A p95 latency target exists. Policy keeps processing inside the EU.","constraints":["Traffic is steady","EU processing boundary","Latency variance must be low"],"unknowns":["What is the prompt cache rate?","Is capacity available in the target region?","What does the model's increment of PTUs look like?"],"question":"How do you buy capacity?","options":[{"name":"Data Zone Provisioned sized for the baseline, spillover to Data Zone Standard","verdict":"best","why":"Steady volume keeps PTUs busy, the zone satisfies residency, and spillover covers peaks.","tradeoff":"Idle cost if volume drops, and capacity must exist before you commit."},{"name":"Global Standard","verdict":"poor","why":"Global may process outside the EU, which breaks the boundary.","tradeoff":"Right when residency is not a constraint and traffic is variable."},{"name":"Priority processing in a Data Zone","verdict":"good","why":"No commitment and better latency than plain Standard.","tradeoff":"Only a p50 target per 5 minutes, and a price premium at steady volume."}],"flips":[{"if":"Traffic falls to a few hundred requests a day","then":"Drop PTUs for Data Zone Standard."},{"if":"A prefix redesign lifts the cache rate to 50%","then":"Resize down: the sizing example falls from 110 to 80 PTUs."}],"takeaway":"Steady, residency-bound traffic favours provisioned capacity with spillover."},{"id":"w16-t05-s2","title":"A business-hours copilot","situation":"A copilot is used from 08:00 to 18:00 on weekdays with sharp morning and post-lunch bursts. Latency matters during bursts. The team wants no multi-month commitment.","constraints":["Spiky, business-hours traffic","No commitment wanted","Global processing allowed"],"unknowns":["How strict is the latency target?","What is the peak-to-average ratio?"],"question":"Which mode fits?","options":[{"name":"Priority processing on a Global Standard deployment","verdict":"best","why":"It targets stable token rates during bursts without capacity held overnight.","tradeoff":"Premium price, and the target is a p50 per 5 minutes, not a per-request guarantee."},{"name":"Plain Global Standard","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest, fine if bursts are mild.","tradeoff":"Latency and 429s vary at peaks."},{"name":"PTU sized for the burst peak","verdict":"poor","why":"Capacity is paid for at night and weekends and sits idle.","tradeoff":"Right when traffic is high and steady all day."}],"flips":[{"if":"A hard latency SLA is contractual","then":"Provisioned capacity with spillover."},{"if":"Usage becomes 24 by 7","then":"Re-run the PTU sizing and compare with reservations."}],"takeaway":"Bursty traffic is a Priority case. Provisioned capacity is for steady load."},{"id":"w16-t05-s3","title":"A pilot heading to production","situation":"A pilot has a few hundred users. Production is expected to be ten times larger, but nobody has measured token mix, cache rate or peak RPM.","constraints":["Traffic unknown","Budget approval needs a number","Three months to launch"],"unknowns":["Can the pilot log RPM and token counts per request?","What is the lead time for PTU capacity in the target region?"],"question":"What is the plan?","options":[{"name":"Stay on Global Standard, instrument tokens and cache rate, size PTUs from measured peaks","verdict":"best","why":"The sizing formula needs peak RPM, prompt and response tokens and cache rate, all of which the pilot can measure.","tradeoff":"Costs a pilot cycle before any commitment saving."},{"name":"Buy a one-year reservation up front based on projected users","verdict":"poor","why":"A reservation does not guarantee capacity and the sizing inputs are guesses.","tradeoff":"Right once utilization is measured and stable."},{"name":"Use Flex processing for production","verdict":"poor","why":"Flex has no SLA and can return 429 for lack of capacity, so interactive users suffer.","tradeoff":"Right for delay-tolerant work only."}],"flips":[{"if":"Measured utilization is steady above a PTU break-even","then":"Move the baseline to PTUs and spill over."},{"if":"Launch needs capacity guarantees in three weeks","then":"Create the provisioned deployment early to prove capacity exists."}],"takeaway":"Measure first, then size. Do not commit on projections."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t05-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Peak 600 RPM, 500-token prompts, 100-token responses, output-to-input ratio 4, cache rate 40%, 2,500 input TPM per PTU, increment 5. How many PTUs?","answer":170,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"PTUs","hint":"Normalized TPM first, then divide and round up to a multiple of 5","explain":"Normalized TPM = 600 x 500 x 0.6 + 4 x 600 x 100 = 180,000 + 240,000 = 420,000. Divided by 2,500 is 168, rounded up to the increment of 5 gives 170 [[r7]].","difficulty":3},{"id":"w16-t05-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about PTU are correct?","options":["Capacity is held whether or not requests arrive","A reservation guarantees capacity in a region","Cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity","Quota does not guarantee capacity"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Provisioned capacity bills while idle, cached tokens are free of PTU use, and quota is not capacity. Reservations also do not guarantee capacity, so create the deployment first [[r6]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A workload moves from no residency rule to an EU-only rule. Which change follows?","options":["Move to Flex processing","Move from Global Standard to Data Zone (EU) types","Raise quota on Global Standard"],"answer":1,"explain":"Data Zone keeps processing inside the zone. Flex and quota do not change where data is processed [[r5]].","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does PTU quota guarantee capacity?","back":"No. Quota and reservations do not guarantee capacity. Create the deployment first."},{"front":"How does spillover work?","back":"Non-200 overflow from a full PTU deployment goes to a standard deployment in the same resource, for all requests or per request via `x-ms-spillover-deployment`."},{"front":"What is Priority processing's latency target?","back":"A p50 token rate per 5-minute window by model, for example 80 TPS for gpt-5.6-sol. It is a target, not a per-request guarantee."}],"related":["w16-t04","w16-t06","w16-t11","w16-l3","w12-t19"],"refs":["r5","r6","r7","r10"]},{"id":"w16-t06","order":6,"group":"Capacity","title":"Batch, flex and async","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Batch and Flex processing trade time for a 50% lower price. Batch queues a file for a 24-hour target, and Flex keeps a synchronous API shape but can be preempted.","useIf":"Nobody waits for the result: evaluations, enrichment, embeddings, classification, backfills.","avoidIf":"A user is waiting, or you cannot tolerate 429, 408 or a result hours later.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Batch** takes a file of requests and returns results later. Azure Global Batch and Data Zone Batch cost 50% less than Global Standard, aim to finish within 24 hours, use a separate enqueued-token quota and carry no real-time SLA [[r5]]. OpenAI's Batch API is also 50% off with its own higher limits and a 24-hour window; the input is a `.jsonl` file with a unique `custom_id` per line, one model per file, up to 200 MB, and the queue counts as pending input tokens [[r12]]. Anthropic Message Batches take up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB, most finish within an hour, and they expire after 24 hours with results kept 29 days [[r13]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Flex** is a per-request tier: set `service_tier: \"flex\"`. On Azure it is a preview for Global Standard at 50% off, with cached-token discounts, no latency or service SLA and preemptible capacity, so a 429 can mean no capacity and a 408 means raise the client timeout (doc samples use 900 seconds). There is no automatic fallback to Standard, it shares quota with Standard, and from 2026-09-25 unsupported models return 400 [[r11]]. OpenAI lists Flex as a beta at Batch API rates, with prompt-caching discounts on top, and its example raises the client timeout to 15 minutes [[tr9]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Choosing a tier by how long the caller can wait","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Seconds","md":"Standard, Priority or provisioned. A person is waiting.","tag":"sync"},{"title":"Minutes","md":"Flex with your own retry and fallback to Standard. Tolerates 429 and 408.","tag":"preview"},{"title":"Hours","md":"Batch with `custom_id` mapping and partial-failure replay.","tag":"async"}]}],"whereToUse":["Offline evaluation runs over a golden set","Document enrichment, tagging and summarisation backfills","Embedding a corpus during re-indexing","Nightly classification of tickets or logs","Synthetic data generation for tests"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The result is consumed hours later","Volume is large and a half-price rate matters","You can isolate the work from interactive quota","Partial failures can be replayed by `custom_id`"],"avoid":["Interactive requests","Work that must finish by a hard deadline shorter than the batch window","Flex where a 429 or 408 breaks the pipeline and no fallback exists"],"flips":["The deadline tightens to minutes: Flex or Standard replaces Batch","Flex is preview on Azure: a production dependency needs a fallback to Standard","The model has no batch support: use Standard with an off-peak rate limiter"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Async options compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Batch","Hours of slack, huge volume","Any deadline under a few hours","24-hour target, no real-time SLA, queue quota"],["Flex (preview on Azure)","Minutes to hours of slack, simple code","You cannot handle preemption","429 means no capacity, 408 needs long timeouts, shares Standard quota [[r11]]"],["Standard off-peak","Model lacks batch support","Peak quota is tight","Full price"],["Queue with workers on Standard","You need control over ordering and retries","Cost sensitivity is high","Full price plus queue operations"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A Flex job loses interactive capacity","cause":"Flex shares quota with Standard, so a surge consumes the same allowance","fix":"Cap Flex concurrency, run it off-peak, or use a separate deployment"},{"symptom":"Flex calls fail with 429 even at low volume","cause":"Capacity is preemptible and there is no automatic fallback","fix":"Retry with backoff, then fall back to Standard in your own code"},{"symptom":"Batch finished but outputs do not match inputs","cause":"Results return in no guaranteed order","fix":"Key every request with a unique `custom_id` and join on it; replay only failed ids"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t06-s1","title":"Evaluate a release against 50,000 prompts","situation":"Each release is tested on a golden set of 50,000 prompts. Results are reviewed the next morning.","constraints":["Nobody waits for results","Volume repeats at every release","Interactive traffic shares the account"],"unknowns":["Does the model support Batch on this platform?","Is enqueued-token quota enough for the set?","How are failures replayed?"],"question":"Where does the run go?","options":[{"name":"Batch with `custom_id` keys and a replay step","verdict":"best","why":"Half price, a separate quota that leaves interactive traffic alone, and the deadline is hours.","tradeoff":"No real-time SLA, so a release blocked on results can wait up to 24 hours."},{"name":"Flex processing","verdict":"good","why":"Same discount with synchronous code.","tradeoff":"Preview, preemptible, shares quota with Standard."},{"name":"Priority processing","verdict":"poor","why":"It pays a premium for latency nobody needs.","tradeoff":"Right for interactive bursts."}],"flips":[{"if":"A release gate needs results within 30 minutes","then":"Use Flex with a Standard fallback, or shrink the set."},{"if":"Enqueued-token quota is too small for the whole set","then":"Split jobs and stagger them."}],"takeaway":"If the deadline is hours, Batch is the default."},{"id":"w16-t06-s2","title":"Back-office review within 15 minutes","situation":"Analysts review AI-drafted summaries about 15 minutes after documents arrive. Volume is moderate and a few delayed items are acceptable.","constraints":["Result needed in minutes","Occasional delay is acceptable","Cost matters"],"unknowns":["How are 429 and 408 handled?","Can the code fall back to Standard?"],"question":"What do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Flex with long timeout, retry, and fallback to Standard","verdict":"best","why":"Half price with a synchronous shape, and the fallback protects the 15-minute expectation.","tradeoff":"Preview on Azure, so keep the fallback and track fallback share."},{"name":"Batch","verdict":"poor","why":"A 24-hour target with no SLA can miss a 15-minute expectation.","tradeoff":"Right when the window is hours."},{"name":"Standard","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and predictable.","tradeoff":"Pays full price for slack you do not need."}],"flips":[{"if":"Fallback share to Standard exceeds half","then":"Flex saves little; use Standard."},{"if":"The model does not support Flex (such requests return 400 from 2026-09-25)","then":"Switch to Standard or Batch by deadline."}],"takeaway":"Minutes of slack fit Flex. Hours fit Batch."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t06-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order these by how long the caller can wait, shortest first.","items":["Standard or Priority","Flex","Batch"],"explain":"Interactive tiers serve people waiting. Flex fits minutes of slack and tolerates preemption. Batch fits hours with a 24-hour target [[r5]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w16-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Flex calls on Azure return 408. What does the doc advise?","options":["Switch the deployment to PTU","Retry immediately without backoff","Raise the client timeout; samples use 900 seconds"],"answer":2,"explain":"On Azure Flex a 408 means raise the timeout, while a 429 can mean no capacity [[r11]]. PTU is unrelated, and immediate retries add load.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t06-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are true of the OpenAI Batch API?","options":["50% discount","One model per file","Results return in input order","Each line needs a unique `custom_id`"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The guide describes a 50% discount, one model per file and unique `custom_id` values. Ordering is not guaranteed, which is why `custom_id` exists [[r12]].","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do Batch and Flex give and take?","back":"About 50% off in exchange for time. Batch has a 24-hour target. Flex is synchronous but preemptible with no SLA."},{"front":"Why can Flex hurt production?","back":"It shares quota with Standard and has no automatic fallback, so a surge or a 429 needs your own handling."},{"front":"Why does Batch need `custom_id`?","back":"Results are not guaranteed to return in input order. The id joins outputs to inputs and drives replay of failures."}],"related":["w16-t02","w16-t05","w16-t08","w16-l3","w14-t14"],"refs":["r5","r11","r12","r13","tr9"]},{"id":"w16-t07","order":7,"group":"Gateway and resilience","title":"API Management AI gateway","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"An AI gateway in API Management puts token limits, token metrics, semantic caching, content safety, load balancing and a circuit breaker in front of your model deployments, so every team gets one governed endpoint.","useIf":"Several apps or teams share model capacity and you need per-consumer limits, cost attribution and failover in one place.","avoidIf":"One app calls one deployment and the gateway would be a new single point of failure with no policy to enforce.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The gateway covers token limits, token metrics, semantic caching, content safety, load balancing and a circuit breaker [[r1]]. **`llm-token-limit`** limits by a `counter-key` using `tokens-per-minute`, a `token-quota` over a `token-quota-period` (`Hourly`, `Daily`, `Weekly`, `Monthly`, `Yearly`), or both. A rate breach returns 429 and a quota breach returns 403. `estimate-prompt-tokens` is required, streaming always estimates, images count up to 1,200 tokens each when estimating, and concurrent requests can briefly exceed the limit. Counters are per gateway, not aggregated across regions. V2 tiers use a token bucket and classic tiers a sliding window [[r2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**`llm-emit-token-metric`** sends token counts to Application Insights with up to 5 custom dimensions per policy, 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active time series per namespace, beyond which data is silently dropped. Streaming needs `include_usage: true` on some OpenAI models for accurate counts, and cached, reasoning and thinking categories are preview [[r3]]. The **semantic cache** uses a paired `llm-semantic-cache-lookup` and `llm-semantic-cache-store`, with `score-threshold`, `embeddings-backend-id`, `ignore-system-messages` and a `vary-by` child; Microsoft advises starting near 0.05, warns above 0.2 may mismatch, and recommends a rate limit after lookup plus content safety with Prompt Shields [[tr1]]. **Backends** carry one `circuitBreaker` rule (`failureCondition`, `tripDuration`, `acceptRetryAfter`); a tripped breaker returns 503. Pools hold up to 30 backends with round-robin, weighted or priority balancing [[r4]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t07-d1","title":"A request path through the AI gateway","alt":"A client request passes semantic cache lookup, token limit and content safety, then a backend pool with a circuit breaker routes to primary or secondary. Outbound, token metrics are emitted and the answer is stored in the cache.","src":"flowchart LR\n  C[Client] --> SC[Cache lookup]\n  SC --> TL[Token limit]\n  TL --> CS[Content safety]\n  CS --> P[Pool with breaker]\n  P --> A[Primary backend]\n  P --> B[Secondary backend]\n  A --> M[Emit token metric]\n  B --> M\n  M --> ST[Cache store]"}],"whereToUse":["A shared platform where several teams call the same model deployments","Per-team token limits and monthly quotas for chargeback","Two or more regional deployments behind one URL with priority routing","Central token metrics in Application Insights for cost dashboards","A public FAQ where a semantic cache is acceptable"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Many consumers share capacity and need fair shares","You want failover and load balancing without changing every client","Cost attribution by team or product is required","Content safety checks must be uniform"],"avoid":["A single small app, where the gateway adds a hop and an outage surface","Long streaming flows that the gateway cannot pass through unbuffered","Semantic caching on permissioned or high-risk answers"],"flips":["Stateful APIs such as Responses need session affinity, so enable it or route statefully","Multi-region traffic: counters are per gateway, so split limits per region or accept overshoot","The Foundry unified model API and AI gateway are preview: use the stable APIM policies for production"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where to put the controls","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["APIM AI gateway","Many consumers, central policy, failover","A single app, tiny scale","Extra hop, single point of failure, limits are approximate [[r1]]"],["SDK and client code","One app, fast iteration","Many clients, inconsistent behaviour","Every team re-implements limits and retries"],["Direct provider quota","Simple, one consumer","Fair shares and failover are needed","No per-consumer control"],["Open-source LLM proxy","You already run one and need provider breadth","You want managed policy and identity integration","You operate and secure it"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Dashboards miss some teams or models","cause":"Metric dimensions exceeded 100 values or 1,000 time series and data was dropped silently [[r3]]","fix":"Use low-cardinality dimensions such as team and model, never user id"},{"symptom":"Limits are exceeded briefly or overcounted","cause":"Token counting is estimated, streaming always estimates and concurrent requests overshoot; images count up to 1,200 tokens each [[r2]]","fix":"Leave headroom, validate against provider usage and do not treat the limit as exact billing"},{"symptom":"A tripped breaker looks like a model outage","cause":"The gateway returns 503 when the circuit is open","fix":"Tag gateway-originated errors in logs and traces and alert on open time"},{"symptom":"A cache hit returns another team's answer","cause":"`vary-by` and the cache key ignored tenant, model or prompt version","fix":"Partition by those fields or turn the cache off for that API"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t07-s1","title":"Five teams, one deployment","situation":"Five product teams share Azure OpenAI deployments. Finance wants cost by team, and a noisy job keeps starving others.","constraints":["One shared quota","Chargeback by team required","Latency overhead must stay small"],"unknowns":["Do all teams use streaming?","Are limits needed per region?","What dimensions are needed on metrics?"],"question":"What do you put in front of the deployments?","options":[{"name":"APIM with `llm-token-limit` per team and `llm-emit-token-metric` by team and model","verdict":"best","why":"Per-consumer rate and quota limits plus attributed metrics solve both problems in one place.","tradeoff":"Estimated counts and brief overshoot; one more hop to operate."},{"name":"A deployment per team","verdict":"good","why":"Strong isolation.","tradeoff":"Fragments quota and still needs central cost reporting."},{"name":"Ask the noisy team to add client-side limits","verdict":"poor","why":"It depends on goodwill and gives no central view.","tradeoff":"Fine for a single app with no sharing."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only one team remains","then":"Drop the gateway and use direct quota."},{"if":"Teams run in multiple regions","then":"Set per-region limits, since counters are not aggregated."}],"takeaway":"A shared model needs per-consumer limits and metrics at one gate."},{"id":"w16-t07-s2","title":"Two regions behind one URL","situation":"A production assistant uses two Azure OpenAI resources in two regions. The primary sometimes returns 429 with a very long Retry-After.","constraints":["Clients cannot change","Failover must be automatic","Data may stay in either region"],"unknowns":["Is the API stateful and needing affinity?","Is the gateway tier Consumption, which lacks circuit breakers?"],"question":"How do you configure it?","options":[{"name":"Priority pool with a circuit breaker that accepts Retry-After","verdict":"best","why":"The breaker trips on failures, honours a long Retry-After and sends traffic to the secondary, which is used only while the primary is tripped.","tradeoff":"Breaker rules are approximate across gateway instances, and the secondary starts with a cold prompt cache."},{"name":"Round-robin across both regions","verdict":"good","why":"Spreads load.","tradeoff":"Sends traffic to a failing region and splits the prompt cache all the time."},{"name":"Retry the primary at the gateway until it answers","verdict":"poor","why":"A long Retry-After means minutes or hours of wasted attempts and added load.","tradeoff":"Right for brief transient blips."}],"flips":[{"if":"The API is stateful","then":"Add session affinity so a conversation sticks to one backend."},{"if":"The tier is Consumption","then":"Circuit breakers are unavailable, so use another tier or client-side failover."}],"takeaway":"Priority pools plus a breaker give automatic regional failover."},{"id":"w16-t07-s3","title":"A semantic cache on an HR assistant","situation":"A team proposes the gateway semantic cache for an HR assistant that answers from permissioned policy documents and employee records.","constraints":["Answers depend on who is asking","Wrong answers carry legal risk","Repeat rate is high"],"unknowns":["Can the cache key carry entitlement scope?","How fast do policies change?"],"question":"Do you enable it?","options":[{"name":"No semantic cache; use prompt caching and token limits","verdict":"best","why":"Microsoft warns cached results can be incorrect, outdated or unsafe, and permissioned answers cannot be shared across users.","tradeoff":"Gives up whole-call savings on repeated questions."},{"name":"Enable it with a strict threshold and `vary-by` on user id","verdict":"good","why":"Scoping by user removes cross-user leakage.","tradeoff":"Hit rate collapses, so the embedding cost per miss may exceed the savings."},{"name":"Enable it with a loose threshold for the best hit rate","verdict":"poor","why":"Loose matches return wrong answers and may cross users.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for public, low-risk content."}],"flips":[{"if":"The corpus is a public FAQ","then":"A strict-threshold semantic cache becomes the best option."},{"if":"Answers are identical for a whole entitlement group","then":"Partition by group and reconsider."}],"takeaway":"Semantic caching is for shared, low-risk answers."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Clients see 503 from the gateway while the model deployment is healthy. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The backend circuit breaker is tripped","The token quota was exceeded","The semantic cache missed"],"answer":0,"explain":"A tripped backend breaker returns 503 [[r4]]. A rate breach returns 429, a quota breach returns 403 [[r2]], and a cache miss falls through to the model.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t07-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each status or limit to its source.","pairs":[{"l":"429 from `llm-token-limit`","r":"Rate limit exceeded"},{"l":"403 from `llm-token-limit`","r":"Token quota exceeded"},{"l":"503 from a backend","r":"Circuit breaker tripped"},{"l":"1,000 active time series","r":"Token metric namespace limit before silent drops"}],"explain":"The three codes come from three different mechanisms, which is why gateway errors should be labelled in traces [[r2]] [[r4]] [[r3]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which practices reduce the risk of a semantic cache returning the wrong user's answer?","options":["Partition with `vary-by` on tenant or entitlement scope","Include model and prompt-template version in the key","Raise the `score-threshold` to be looser","Skip the cache for permissioned content"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Partitioning, versioned keys and skipping permissioned content reduce leakage. A looser threshold (higher in APIM) produces more wrong matches [[tr1]].","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do `llm-token-limit` return on breach?","back":"429 for the tokens-per-minute rate, 403 for the token quota. Counters are per gateway, not aggregated across regions."},{"front":"What happens when a backend breaker trips?","back":"The gateway returns 503. Rules are approximate across instances and unavailable in the Consumption tier. Use `acceptRetryAfter` for long Retry-After values."},{"front":"What APIM metric limits exist?","back":"5 custom dimensions per policy, 100 unique values per dimension, 1,000 active time series per namespace. Beyond that data is dropped silently."}],"related":["w16-t04","w16-t09","w16-t11","w16-l2","w15-t04"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","tr1"]},{"id":"w16-t08","order":8,"group":"Gateway and resilience","title":"Retries, timeouts and jitter","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A retry spends more of the server's capacity to raise your odds of success. Done once, with backoff, jitter, a cap and a deadline, it absorbs blips. Done at every layer, it turns a blip into an outage.","useIf":"Calls fail transiently with 429, 408 or 5xx, and you must decide what to retry, how long to wait and when to stop.","avoidIf":"The error is a quota or billing failure, a client error, or a side-effecting call with no idempotency key.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"OpenAI's guidance is to treat `Retry-After` as a minimum plus a small random delay, otherwise use exponential backoff with jitter, cap attempts and total time, never retry quota or billing errors, and count SDK retries so loops do not multiply [[r16]]. AWS explains why: capped backoff alone leaves clients retrying in clusters, and **jitter** spreads them out and cuts total calls [[r18]]. Its **full jitter** variant sleeps a random time between 0 and the capped exponential value."},{"t":"p","md":"**Layers multiply.** With three retries at each of five stacked layers, a failing database sees 3^5 = 243 times the load, which makes recovery unlikely. Retry at one point in the stack, and limit retries locally with a token bucket [[tr3]]. **Timeouts** come from the downstream latency percentile: choose an acceptable false-timeout rate such as 0.1% and read the p99.9 latency, padded when the percentile is close to the median [[tr3]]. For LLM calls use separate limits for time to first token and for total generation, since output length drives total time. Only **idempotent** calls are safe to retry; a tool that charges a card needs an idempotency key first [[tr3]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"One retry layer with full jitter, Retry-After and a deadline (illustrative)","code":"def call_with_retries(send, max_attempts=4, base=0.5, cap=20.0, deadline_s=60.0):\n    start = time.monotonic()\n    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n        resp = send()\n        if resp.ok:\n            return resp\n        if resp.status in (400, 401, 403, 404) or resp.is_quota_error:\n            raise ApiError(resp)  # never retry\n        wait = random.uniform(0, min(cap, base * 2 ** attempt))  # full jitter\n        wait = max(wait, resp.retry_after_s or 0)  # Retry-After is a minimum\n        if time.monotonic() - start + wait > deadline_s:\n            break\n        time.sleep(wait)\n    raise GiveUp()","caption":"Count SDK retries too: set the SDK's own retries to zero if this loop owns the policy."},{"t":"table","caption":"What to retry","head":["Signal","Retry?","Why"],"rows":[["429 from a rate window, `Retry-After` present","Yes, after at least that delay","Transient; the server told you when"],["429 for exhausted quota or billing","No","More attempts cannot succeed [[r16]]"],["408, 500, 502, 503, 504","Yes, with jitter and a cap","Often transient; 503 from a gateway may be a tripped breaker"],["400, 401, 403, 404","No","The same request will fail again"]]}],"whereToUse":["The single client wrapper around model calls in your service","Gateway policies that retry across backends","Agent frameworks that retry failed steps or tool calls","Batch and Flex clients that wait on 429 and 408 [[r11]]","Embedding and ingestion workers under rate limits"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Failures are transient and calls are idempotent","You can bound attempts and total time","The server provides `Retry-After`"],"avoid":["Retrying quota, billing or validation errors","Retrying inside every layer of a deep call stack","Retrying side-effecting tool calls without an idempotency key","Retrying after a stream has already shown text to the user, unless you can resume"],"flips":["Sustained overload: stop retrying and shed load or degrade","Deadline shorter than the backoff: fail fast and fall back","Hedging is acceptable instead: duplicates a cheap idempotent call early rather than waiting"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Failure responses compared","head":["Response","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Retry with jitter","Brief transient failures","Overload, quota exhaustion","Amplifies load if layered"],["Circuit breaker","A dependency is down","Single brief blips","Rejects traffic when open; 503s"],["Fallback model or region","Capacity or regional trouble","Quality gap is large","Cold cache, residency, different behaviour"],["Fail fast and tell the user","Deadline cannot be met","Work is cheap and likely to succeed","A visible failure"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A small provider blip becomes a long outage","cause":"SDK, framework and app each retry three times, multiplying traffic","fix":"Keep one retry layer, set the others to zero and cap total attempts and time"},{"symptom":"Thousands of clients return at the same second","cause":"Backoff without jitter keeps clients synchronised","fix":"Add full jitter and honour `Retry-After` as a minimum"},{"symptom":"A customer was charged twice","cause":"A tool call was retried after a timeout and was not idempotent","fix":"Send an idempotency key and check state before repeating side effects"},{"symptom":"Retries keep failing with the same 429","cause":"The error was exhausted quota or billing","fix":"Detect it, stop retrying, alert and degrade"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t08-s1","title":"Three layers, three retries each","situation":"A service calls a framework that calls an SDK that calls a model. Each layer makes up to three attempts with no jitter. During a provider slowdown the load on the model endpoint multiplies and recovery drags on.","constraints":["Interactive traffic","Provider returns 429 and 503","Layers owned by different teams"],"unknowns":["Which layer should own the policy?","What is the user's deadline?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"One retry layer with jitter, `Retry-After` honoured, capped attempts and a total deadline; other layers set to zero","verdict":"best","why":"Three attempts at each of three layers is up to 27 attempts per request at the bottom. One owned policy removes the multiplication.","tradeoff":"One team owns retry behaviour and must expose it as configuration."},{"name":"Raise the base delay in every layer","verdict":"good","why":"Slows the storm.","tradeoff":"Layers still multiply and latency grows."},{"name":"Add more retries to be safe","verdict":"poor","why":"More attempts add load when the dependency is overloaded.","tradeoff":"More retries are right only for rare transient faults."}],"flips":[{"if":"The deadline is under a second","then":"No retries; use a fallback or fail fast."},{"if":"Overload persists","then":"Add a circuit breaker and shed load."}],"takeaway":"Retry at one layer. Layers multiply."},{"id":"w16-t08-s2","title":"The stream breaks halfway","situation":"A chat UI streams tokens. After 300 tokens the connection drops with a 5xx.","constraints":["The user has seen partial text","Generation is not idempotent in content","A deterministic resume is not available"],"unknowns":["Can the client resume from a partial state?","Is the response stored server-side?"],"question":"What should the client do?","options":[{"name":"Keep the partial text visible, mark it incomplete and let the user retry or continue","verdict":"best","why":"A silent restart replaces text the user has read and doubles cost.","tradeoff":"One extra user action."},{"name":"Retry the whole call and replace the text","verdict":"good","why":"Often fine when little text was shown.","tradeoff":"The new answer can differ and the first tokens are paid twice."},{"name":"Retry in a loop until the stream completes","verdict":"poor","why":"Repeated partial runs waste tokens and risk a retry storm.","tradeoff":"Right only with a hard cap and when no text has been shown yet."}],"flips":[{"if":"Background mode stores the response server-side","then":"Poll or resume it rather than regenerating."},{"if":"No text was displayed yet","then":"A bounded retry is fine."}],"takeaway":"After the user has seen output, retrying changes their experience."},{"id":"w16-t08-s3","title":"An agent that issues refunds","situation":"An agent calls a refund tool that times out at the client. The refund may or may not have been processed.","constraints":["The tool has side effects","Money moves","Idempotency keys are supported by the payment API"],"unknowns":["Does the API return the prior result for a repeated key?","Is there a status lookup?"],"question":"How do you retry?","options":[{"name":"Retry with the same idempotency key, or look up status before repeating","verdict":"best","why":"The key makes repeated attempts safe, so the side effect happens once.","tradeoff":"Requires key generation, storage and a status endpoint."},{"name":"Do not retry and escalate to a human","verdict":"good","why":"Safe for rare failures.","tradeoff":"Slow and costly at volume."},{"name":"Retry blindly with backoff","verdict":"poor","why":"A double refund is possible.","tradeoff":"Blind retries are right only for read-only calls."}],"flips":[{"if":"The API offers no idempotency or status","then":"Escalate for human reconciliation."},{"if":"The call is a read","then":"Retry freely within the budget."}],"takeaway":"Side effects need idempotency before retries."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t08-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Five stacked layers each retry up to 3 times when the bottom service fails. How many times the original load can the bottom service see?","answer":243,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"x","explain":"Retries multiply: 3^5 = 243. AWS uses this example to argue for retrying at a single point in the stack [[tr3]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t08-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"What is the main flaw in this retry policy?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"for attempt in range(10):\n    r = send()\n    if r.ok: return r\n    time.sleep(2 ** attempt)"},"options":["It sleeps too short","It uses exponential backoff","No jitter, no cap, no deadline, and it retries every error"],"answer":2,"explain":"Without jitter clients stay synchronised, the delay is uncapped (2^9 is 512 seconds), there is no total deadline and quota or 4xx errors are retried too. Exponential backoff itself is fine [[r18]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t08-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which failures are reasonable to retry with backoff and a cap?","options":["429 from a rate window with Retry-After","503 from an overloaded endpoint","429 for exhausted billing quota","400 for a malformed request"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Transient rate and overload errors can succeed later. Quota or billing exhaustion and 400s fail again [[r16]].","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is full jitter?","back":"Sleep a random time between 0 and min(cap, base x 2^attempt). It spreads retries and cuts total calls versus no jitter."},{"front":"Why retry at only one layer?","back":"Retries multiply across layers. Three retries at five layers is 3^5 = 243 times the load on the bottom service."},{"front":"How should `Retry-After` be used?","back":"As a minimum wait, plus a small random delay. Never retry quota or billing errors."}],"related":["w16-t04","w16-t09","w16-t10","w16-l4","w01-t13"],"refs":["r16","r18","r11","tr3"]},{"id":"w16-t09","order":9,"group":"Gateway and resilience","title":"Circuit breakers, bulkheads and hedging","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A circuit breaker stops calls to a failing dependency, a bulkhead stops one consumer from sinking the rest, and hedging sends an early duplicate to cut tail latency. Each trades capacity, cost or complexity for isolation or speed.","useIf":"A failing or slow dependency, or one noisy consumer, can drag down everything else.","avoidIf":"Traffic is small and failures are rare: simple retries and good timeouts are enough.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **circuit breaker** has three states. In **closed** it passes calls and counts recent failures. When failures exceed a threshold it moves to **open** and rejects calls quickly. After a trip period it enters **half-open**, lets a few calls through and closes again only if they succeed [[tr4]]. In API Management a backend has one `circuitBreaker` rule with `failureCondition`, `tripDuration` and `acceptRetryAfter`; a tripped breaker returns 503, rules are approximate across gateway instances, and the Consumption tier lacks them. For Azure OpenAI backends accept `Retry-After`, which can be very long [[r4]]. AWS warns breakers add modal behaviour that is hard to test and can slow recovery, so pair them with a local token bucket on retries [[tr3]]."},{"t":"p","md":"A **bulkhead** partitions capacity into pools so a fault stays inside its own pool. Azure's pattern partitions instances by consumer load and availability needs [[tr5]]. For models that means separate deployments or per-consumer limits for batch, interactive and experimental workloads; Azure advises separate deployments because mixed workloads share batches and cache [[r27]]. **Hedging** sends a duplicate after about the 95th-percentile delay and takes the first reply. In a Google BigTable benchmark it cut the 99.9th percentile for 1,000 key reads from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for about 2% extra requests [[r19]] (figures from a search summary, PDF not parsed)."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t09-d1","title":"Circuit breaker states","alt":"Closed moves to open when failures exceed a threshold. Open moves to half-open after the trip duration. Half-open returns to closed if probes succeed or to open if one fails.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Closed\n  Closed --> Open: failures exceed threshold\n  Open --> HalfOpen: trip duration ends\n  HalfOpen --> Closed: probes succeed\n  HalfOpen --> Open: a probe fails"},{"t":"table","caption":"Which tool for which failure","head":["Failure","Tool","Why"],"rows":[["A dependency is down or very slow","Circuit breaker","Fail fast, give it room to recover"],["One consumer exhausts shared capacity","Bulkhead","Cap its pool, protect the others"],["A rare slow call on a cheap, idempotent request","Hedging","Duplicate early, cancel the loser"]]}],"whereToUse":["APIM backend pools across two model regions","Separate deployments for batch, interactive and experimental traffic","Per-tool concurrency limits in an agent runtime","Hedged calls to embeddings, reranking or short classification","Dependency calls from retrieval to search, storage or tools"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Breaker: a dependency can fail for minutes and callers would otherwise pile up","Bulkhead: several workloads with different value share capacity","Hedging: tail latency hurts, the call is cheap, idempotent and short"],"avoid":["Hedging long generations or tool calls with side effects: it doubles spend and risks duplicates","A breaker with a trip duration shorter than the dependency's recovery time","Hedging when the cause is overload: duplicates add load"],"flips":["Provider returns long Retry-After: set `acceptRetryAfter` so the breaker honours it","Hedge rate exceeds a small budget: stop hedging and fix the tail","One tenant dominates traffic: give it a pool of its own"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Isolation and speed tools compared","head":["Tool","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Retry with jitter","Blips","Sustained failure or overload","Amplifies load"],["Circuit breaker","Sustained dependency failure","Short blips; gateway fleets with approximate counts","503s while open; modal behaviour [[tr3]]"],["Bulkhead","Noisy neighbours, mixed criticality","Scarce capacity","Fragmented quota and idle headroom"],["Hedging","Slow tail on cheap idempotent calls","Long, costly or side-effecting calls","Extra spend and load on the duplicate"],["Load shedding or degradation","Capacity is exhausted","Everything is critical","Users lose features (see the fallback topic)"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Callers see 503 with no sign of an outage","cause":"The gateway breaker is open and the error is gateway-originated","fix":"Tag gateway errors in traces, alert on breaker open time and show it on the dashboard"},{"symptom":"The breaker flaps open and closed","cause":"Trip duration is short and half-open probes hit a still-sick dependency","fix":"Lengthen or back off the trip duration; accept Retry-After for Azure OpenAI backends"},{"symptom":"Hedging raised the bill and the error rate","cause":"Duplicates were sent for long generations during an overload","fix":"Hedge only cheap idempotent calls, cap the hedge rate and cancel the losing request"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t09-s1","title":"A batch job crowds out chat","situation":"A nightly pipeline and an interactive chat share one deployment. When the pipeline runs, chat users see slow replies and 429s.","constraints":["Both workloads are valuable","One shared quota","The pipeline can move or shrink"],"unknowns":["Can the pipeline use Batch?","Is there a gateway to enforce per-consumer limits?"],"question":"Which isolation fits?","options":[{"name":"Bulkhead: separate deployments, or per-consumer token limits, and Batch for the pipeline","verdict":"best","why":"The failure is contention, not a dependency outage. Isolating pools protects chat directly.","tradeoff":"Quota is fragmented and each pool needs headroom."},{"name":"A circuit breaker on the pipeline","verdict":"poor","why":"Breakers react to failures of a dependency, not to a consumer using too much capacity.","tradeoff":"Right when the model endpoint itself fails."},{"name":"Hedge chat requests","verdict":"poor","why":"Duplicates add load to a saturated deployment.","tradeoff":"Right for a rare slow tail with spare capacity."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only a few teams and low volume","then":"A single deployment with a simple limiter may do."},{"if":"The pipeline can wait hours","then":"Batch makes the isolation nearly free."}],"takeaway":"Contention needs a bulkhead, not a breaker."},{"id":"w16-t09-s2","title":"A slow tail on reranking","situation":"A reranking call is cheap, idempotent and usually answers in 80 ms, but 1 in 100 calls takes over 1 second and blows the retrieval budget.","constraints":["Latency budget for retrieval is 400 ms","Spare capacity exists","Calls are read-only"],"unknowns":["Why is the tail slow: queueing, a cold node or overload?","Can the loser be cancelled?"],"question":"What do you apply?","options":[{"name":"Hedge after about the p95 delay, with a hedge budget and cancellation","verdict":"best","why":"The call is cheap and idempotent, so an early duplicate trims the tail for a few percent extra requests.","tradeoff":"Extra load and cost on the hedged calls."},{"name":"Retry after a 1 second timeout","verdict":"good","why":"Cuts the worst tail.","tradeoff":"The user already waited the full timeout."},{"name":"Open a circuit breaker on slow responses","verdict":"poor","why":"A one-percent tail is not a dependency outage and the breaker would reject healthy traffic.","tradeoff":"Right for sustained high error or latency."}],"flips":[{"if":"The tail is caused by overload","then":"Hedging adds load; fix capacity first."},{"if":"The call is a long generation","then":"Do not hedge; use a deadline and fallback."}],"takeaway":"Hedge cheap, idempotent calls with spare capacity."},{"id":"w16-t09-s3","title":"Primary region keeps failing with long Retry-After","situation":"The primary region returns 429 and 503 with Retry-After values of many minutes. A secondary region exists behind the gateway.","constraints":["Automatic failover needed","Clients cannot change","Gateway tier supports breakers"],"unknowns":["Does the API need session affinity?","Does the secondary have quota?"],"question":"How should the gateway behave?","options":[{"name":"Priority pool with a breaker that accepts Retry-After and a warmed secondary","verdict":"best","why":"The breaker stays open for the advertised time and traffic flows to the secondary without client changes.","tradeoff":"The secondary starts with a cold cache and needs quota."},{"name":"Round-robin both regions","verdict":"good","why":"Spreads load.","tradeoff":"Keeps sending half the traffic to a failing region."},{"name":"Hedge every request across both regions","verdict":"poor","why":"It doubles cost and load permanently to mask a regional fault.","tradeoff":"Right only for rare tail events on cheap calls."}],"flips":[{"if":"The API is stateful","then":"Add session affinity or accept losing state on failover."},{"if":"The secondary lacks quota","then":"Fix capacity first, or degrade features."}],"takeaway":"A breaker with accepted Retry-After plus a ready secondary fails over cleanly."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t09-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the life of a circuit breaker after a dependency starts failing.","items":["Closed: failures are counted","Open: calls are rejected quickly","Half-open: a few probe calls pass","Closed again: probes succeeded"],"explain":"Failures push it open. After the trip duration, half-open allows probes. Success closes it, failure reopens it [[tr4]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w16-t09-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"You hedge any request still unanswered after the p95 latency. At most what percentage of requests send a duplicate?","answer":5,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"%","explain":"By definition 5% of requests take longer than p95, so at most about 5% trigger a hedge; cancelling the loser and faster replies can bring it lower, as in the BigTable benchmark's roughly 2% [[r19]]. Derived.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t09-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"The cause of slowness is overload on the dependency. What does hedging do?","options":["Reduces load by caching","Adds load and can worsen the slowdown","Opens the breaker"],"answer":1,"explain":"Duplicates add requests to an already saturated service. Hedging suits a slow tail with spare capacity, not overload. It does not cache or trip breakers.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the breaker states?","back":"Closed (count failures), open (reject fast), half-open (probe with a few calls). APIM returns 503 when open."},{"front":"Bulkhead in one line?","back":"Partition capacity into pools so one consumer's fault or load stays inside its own pool."},{"front":"When is hedging safe?","back":"Cheap, idempotent, short calls with spare capacity. Hedge after about p95, cap the hedge rate and cancel the loser."}],"related":["w16-t07","w16-t08","w16-t10","w16-l4","w10-t10"],"refs":["r4","r19","r27","tr3","tr4","tr5"]},{"id":"w16-t10","order":10,"group":"Gateway and resilience","title":"Fallbacks and graceful degradation","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"When the best path fails or capacity runs out, step down a defined ladder: another deployment, a smaller model, fewer features, a cached or retrieval-only answer, then an honest error. Decide the rungs and what you tell users before the incident.","useIf":"A failing model, quota or region must not mean a dead product.","avoidIf":"A degraded answer would be wrong enough to harm users, in which case an honest refusal is the better rung.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **fallback** replaces the failed component with another that does the same job. **Graceful degradation** keeps part of the function when parts fail. Azure's reliability guidance asks for failure detection that starts degraded mode automatically, and for a **degraded user experience that tells users what still works and what changed** [[tr6]]. Platform help is partial: PTU **spillover** sends non-200 overflow to a standard deployment in the same resource [[r7]], but Flex has no automatic fallback to Standard [[r11]], and Foundry provides no automatic failover to another region [[r15]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Design the **ladder** by cost of being wrong. A secondary deployment of the same model keeps behaviour. A smaller model keeps the feature but may break structured output, tool use or the context window. Reduced modes drop tools, shorten output, or answer only from retrieval with citations. The last rungs are a cached answer and a clear error with a retry time. Every rung needs an eval, a guardrail check, and a log tag so degraded traffic is counted, not hidden in success rates."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t10-d1","title":"A degradation ladder","alt":"The request tries the primary model, then a secondary deployment, then a smaller model, then a retrieval-only or cached answer, then an honest error.","src":"flowchart TB\n  A[Primary model] -->|fails| B[Secondary deployment]\n  B -->|fails| C[Smaller model]\n  C -->|fails| D[Retrieval only or cached answer]\n  D -->|fails| E[Honest error with retry time]"},{"t":"table","caption":"What each rung keeps and gives up","head":["Rung","Keeps","Gives up","Tell the user?"],"rows":[["Secondary deployment","Same model, same behaviour","Cold cache, extra latency, maybe residency","Usually no, log it"],["Smaller model","The feature","Quality, tool use, long context","Yes if quality visibly changes"],["Reduced mode (no tools, short output)","Core answers","Actions and depth","Yes"],["Retrieval only or cached answer","Cited facts","Synthesis and freshness","Yes"],["Honest error","Trust","The task","Yes, with when to retry"]]}],"whereToUse":["Customer-facing assistants that must stay up through provider incidents","Agents with write tools, which can drop to read-only or propose-only mode","Regulated assistants, where the safe rung is retrieval with citations or a refusal","Pipelines that fall back from Flex to Standard","Capacity-exhausted peaks, shedding low-priority features first"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A second path exists and passes the same evals and safety checks","Failure rate or capacity pressure is detected automatically","Users are told what changed","Degraded traffic is measured separately"],"avoid":["Falling back to a model with weaker content filters after a policy block","Silent quality drops on high-stakes answers","Fallbacks across a residency boundary you are not allowed to cross"],"flips":["Wrong answers are costly: prefer refusal or retrieval only over a weaker model","Free consumer tier: a smaller model with a banner is acceptable","Agents act on the world: degrade to read-only before degrading quality"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Failure strategies compared","head":["Strategy","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Fail with an honest error","Wrong answers are worse than none","Users expect always-on","Visible downtime"],["Model fallback","A comparable model passes your evals","Output formats, tools or context differ","Prompts tuned for the primary may break"],["Reduced features","Core value survives","Actions are the product","Needs a product decision on what to drop"],["Cached or retrieval-only answer","Knowledge questions, citations suffice","Synthesis or action required","Stale content"],["Queue and retry later","Asynchronous work","A user is waiting","Backlog growth"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Fallback calls fail with context or schema errors","cause":"The secondary model has a smaller context window or different structured-output support","fix":"Test the full ladder in CI with real prompts; keep prompts portable or branch per model"},{"symptom":"A blocked prompt gets answered by the fallback","cause":"The fallback path skips or loosens content filters after the primary rejected the request","fix":"Never fall back on policy blocks; apply the same guardrails to every rung"},{"symptom":"Users complain of worse answers, SLO dashboard is green","cause":"Degraded responses count as success and are not tagged","fix":"Tag degraded mode, track its share, and decide whether degraded counts toward the SLO"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t10-s1","title":"A regulated assistant during an outage","situation":"An assistant gives policy answers to staff in a regulated setting. The primary model is rate limited for 30 minutes. A smaller model has not been evaluated on this corpus.","constraints":["A wrong answer has compliance cost","Citations are required","Retrieval still works"],"unknowns":["Is the smaller model evaluated and approved?","Can retrieval-only answers satisfy the user need?"],"question":"What do you serve during the outage?","options":[{"name":"Retrieval-only mode with citations and a clear banner, or a refusal with a retry time","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps verifiable facts and avoids unevaluated synthesis, and the banner tells users what changed.","tradeoff":"Less helpful: no summarisation or reasoning."},{"name":"Fallback to the unevaluated smaller model","verdict":"poor","why":"Unmeasured quality in a regulated context is the risk you are managing.","tradeoff":"Right after it passes evals and approval, and for low-stakes tiers."},{"name":"Secondary deployment of the same model in another region","verdict":"good","why":"Same behaviour, if residency allows.","tradeoff":"Needs quota and a cold cache, and may cross a boundary."}],"flips":[{"if":"A second region is permitted and has quota","then":"Fail over to it; this keeps behaviour intact."},{"if":"The smaller model is approved","then":"Use it for low-risk intents."}],"takeaway":"In regulated work the safe rung is evidence, not a weaker model."},{"id":"w16-t10-s2","title":"A free-tier consumer chat","situation":"A consumer chat feature has paying and free users. At peak, quota is exhausted and 429s appear.","constraints":["Cost matters","Wrong answers are low-stakes","Paying users must be protected"],"unknowns":["Can traffic be classified by tier at the gateway?","How much headroom does the smaller model have?"],"question":"How do you degrade?","options":[{"name":"Route free users to a smaller model and shorter outputs, keep paying users on the primary, show a banner","verdict":"best","why":"It sheds load where the value is lowest and tells users what changed.","tradeoff":"Free-tier quality drops at peaks and needs tier-aware routing."},{"name":"Return 429 to everyone and let clients retry","verdict":"poor","why":"Retries add load and paying users are hit equally.","tradeoff":"Right only for non-interactive clients that can wait."},{"name":"Queue everything and answer later","verdict":"good","why":"No load shed.","tradeoff":"Chat users will not wait; the queue grows."}],"flips":[{"if":"Quota exhaustion is permanent","then":"Buy capacity (PTU with spillover) rather than degrade daily."},{"if":"Brand risk of weaker answers is high","then":"Reduce features, not quality."}],"takeaway":"Degrade by user value and say so."},{"id":"w16-t10-s3","title":"An action-taking agent under failure","situation":"An agent that can update records and send emails loses its strongest model. A weaker model is available and is more error-prone on tool arguments.","constraints":["Actions have external effects","Some actions are irreversible","Reads are safe"],"unknowns":["Which tools are irreversible?","Is a human approval path available?"],"question":"What does degraded mode look like?","options":[{"name":"Read-only or propose-only mode: reads and drafts continue, writes wait for approval or recovery","verdict":"best","why":"Weaker argument quality is dangerous only when actions execute. Removing writes bounds the blast radius.","tradeoff":"Users lose automation until recovery."},{"name":"Weaker model with all tools enabled","verdict":"poor","why":"Wrong arguments could trigger irreversible actions.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for reversible, low-value tools behind approval."},{"name":"Stop all responses","verdict":"good","why":"Safe and simple.","tradeoff":"Loses read-only value that was still safe."}],"flips":[{"if":"All tools are reversible and low-value","then":"A weaker model may keep running with logging."},{"if":"A human reviews every action anyway","then":"Propose-only becomes the normal path."}],"takeaway":"Degrade authority before you degrade intelligence."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t10-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"The primary model blocks a prompt with a content filter. What should the fallback path do?","options":["Send it to a model with fewer filters","Apply the same guardrails and not retry the blocked request elsewhere","Retry it with higher temperature"],"answer":1,"explain":"A policy block is a decision, not a failure. Falling back to a looser path defeats the control. Temperature does not change policy.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t10-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are sensible degradation rungs for a tool-using agent?","options":["Disable write tools and keep reads","Shorten outputs","Silently enable a model with weaker safety settings","Show a banner explaining reduced features"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Reducing authority, shortening output and telling users are standard. Silently weakening safety is not a degradation but a control bypass [[tr6]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t10-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Flex calls returned 429 for an hour and the pipeline stalled. What was missing?","options":["More quota for Flex","A longer max_tokens","Your own fallback to Standard"],"answer":2,"explain":"Azure Flex has no automatic fallback to Standard, so you must build it [[r11]]. Flex shares quota with Standard, and a larger max_tokens would not help.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Fallback versus graceful degradation?","back":"A fallback swaps in another component of the same kind. Degradation keeps a reduced function, such as read-only mode or retrieval-only answers."},{"front":"What must users be told in degraded mode?","back":"What still works and what changed. Azure's reliability guidance calls for a notification mechanism."},{"front":"Why tag degraded responses?","back":"So dashboards and SLOs count them separately. Otherwise a green success rate hides worse answers."}],"related":["w16-t03","w16-t09","w16-t11","w16-l4","w06-t10"],"refs":["r7","r11","r15","tr6"]},{"id":"w16-t11","order":11,"group":"Gateway and resilience","title":"Regional failover and capacity planning","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Foundry does not fail over for you. You pick a recovery tier, build the second region, size both for the load they must carry, set SLOs with an error budget, and rehearse the switch.","useIf":"A regional incident would break your availability target, or a launch will multiply traffic.","avoidIf":"Your availability target is low enough that a documented hot/cold recovery meets it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry does not provide automatic failover or disaster recovery. Microsoft's guidance is two resources in two regions with two project connections, Service Health alerts and availability tests. It describes three tiers: **hot/hot** with an RTO of minutes and an RPO near zero, **hot/warm** with an RTO of 30 minutes to 2 hours, and **hot/cold** with an RTO of 2 to 8 hours [[r15]]. Global and Data Zone Standard are not a failover: if the primary region has an interruption, all traffic initially routed to it is affected, so they do not replace a second region [[r5]]."},{"t":"p","md":"A second region must hold **quota and capacity**, and quota is not capacity [[r6]]. It starts with a **cold prompt cache**, because caches are per region. APIM counters are per gateway, not aggregated across regions [[r2]]. Residency may limit which region you can use. State kept in the primary resource does not follow you, so keep conversation state in a store you control (author synthesis)."},{"t":"p","md":"Write **SLIs** first: time to first token p95, total latency p95 bucketed by output tokens, success rate excluding client quota errors, 429 and 503 rates, breaker open time, cache hit ratio and cost per successful task (author synthesis from the Azure latency guidance [[r27]]). An **error budget** is the rate at which the SLO may be missed; SLOs of 100% are neither realistic nor useful [[tr7]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Error budget","expr":"budget minutes = (1 - SLO) x minutes in the period","vars":[{"k":"SLO","v":"target success ratio, for example 0.999"},{"k":"period","v":"30 days = 43,200 minutes"}],"example":"99.9% over 30 days leaves 43.2 minutes. A 40-minute regional outage burns 40 / 43.2 = 93% of it. A 99% SLO leaves 432 minutes (derived)."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w16-t11-d1","title":"A hot/warm two-region topology","alt":"A gateway routes to a primary region with a PTU deployment and standard spillover, and to a warm secondary region with its own deployment. Health probes feed the gateway.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[Users] --> G[Gateway]\n  G --> P[Primary region]\n  G -.-> S[Secondary region]\n  P --> PTU[PTU baseline]\n  PTU --> SP[Standard spillover]\n  S --> W[Warm deployment]\n  H[Health probes] --> G"},{"t":"table","caption":"Recovery tiers","head":["Tier","RTO","Cost shape","Fits when"],"rows":[["Hot/hot","Minutes, RPO near zero","Two live regions, each able to carry the load","Tight availability SLO"],["Hot/warm","30 minutes to 2 hours","Secondary deployed but lightly used","Moderate SLO, cost aware"],["Hot/cold","2 to 8 hours","Runbook and quota, deploy on demand","Internal tools with relaxed SLOs"]]}],"whereToUse":["Production assistants with an availability SLO above 99%","Launch planning before traffic multiplies","Quarterly game days that fail the primary region on purpose","Budget reviews that compare failover cost with outage cost","Quota and capacity requests for secondary regions"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The SLO cannot tolerate hours of recovery","A second region is permitted by residency","Load tests show how much capacity each region needs","A drill proves the switch works"],"avoid":["Hot/hot for an internal tool that tolerates a few hours of downtime","Assuming quota or a reservation in the secondary region means capacity","Failing over to a region you have never load tested"],"flips":["SLO tightens from 99% to 99.9%: move from hot/cold to hot/warm or hot/hot","Residency forbids a second region: invest in fallback models and degradation instead","Traffic is 5x at launch: size from a load test and request capacity early"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Availability designs compared","head":["Design","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Single region, retries only","Low SLO, small scale","A regional incident occurs","Downtime equals the incident"],["Hot/cold runbook","SLO of hours is acceptable","Tight SLO","Slow, needs rehearsed steps and quota"],["Hot/warm with gateway failover","Moderate SLO","Both regions hit by the same event","Idle capacity, cold cache"],["Hot/hot across regions","Tight SLO, large traffic","Budget limited, residency bound","Double capacity, split caches, complex state"],["Degradation only (no second region)","Residency blocks a region","Provider outage is total","Reduced function during outages"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Failover works in a drill but the secondary returns 429 under load","cause":"Quota or capacity in the secondary was sized for tests, not for the full peak","fix":"Load test the secondary at full peak and hold quota and capacity for it"},{"symptom":"After failover, latency and cost jump","cause":"The secondary starts with a cold prompt cache and a different deployment mix","fix":"Budget for the jump, prewarm where supported, and watch cache hit ratio during failover"},{"symptom":"Conversations vanish at failover","cause":"State lived in the primary resource","fix":"Keep session state in your own replicated store"},{"symptom":"Failover never triggers","cause":"No health signal watches model errors, only the gateway","fix":"Use availability tests, Service Health alerts and breaker state as inputs"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w16-t11-s1","title":"A 40-minute regional outage","situation":"A regional incident took an assistant offline for 40 minutes. The SLO is 99.9% monthly. The team has one region and Global Standard deployments.","constraints":["99.9% monthly SLO","EU residency applies","Budget exists for modest standby cost"],"unknowns":["Which second EU region has quota and capacity?","Is conversation state external?","What failover time is acceptable?"],"question":"What do you build?","options":[{"name":"Hot/warm in a second EU region behind the gateway, with a drill and a cold-cache budget","verdict":"best","why":"It brings the RTO to minutes to two hours at a standby cost the SLO justifies and keeps residency.","tradeoff":"Idle capacity, a cold cache and a drill schedule."},{"name":"Hot/cold runbook","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest, with a 2 to 8 hour RTO.","tradeoff":"One incident exceeds the monthly budget of 43.2 minutes by hours."},{"name":"Add retries and a longer timeout","verdict":"poor","why":"Retries cannot cross a dead region.","tradeoff":"Retries are right for blips inside a healthy region."}],"flips":[{"if":"The SLO is relaxed to 99%","then":"Hot/cold may be enough."},{"if":"Residency allows any region","then":"Hot/hot across regions becomes feasible."}],"takeaway":"Pick the tier from the SLO arithmetic, then prove it with a drill."},{"id":"w16-t11-s2","title":"An internal tool with a relaxed SLO","situation":"An internal research assistant serves 200 staff during working hours. The business accepts up to a few hours of downtime a month.","constraints":["99% SLO","Small budget","Single geography"],"unknowns":["Is there a documented owner for incidents?","Is quota in the backup region pre-approved?"],"question":"How much resilience do you buy?","options":[{"name":"Hot/cold: a runbook, pre-approved quota and an availability test","verdict":"best","why":"A 99% SLO allows 432 minutes a month, so a 2 to 8 hour recovery fits the arithmetic at low cost.","tradeoff":"Slow recovery and a manual step."},{"name":"Hot/hot across two regions","verdict":"poor","why":"Double capacity for a tolerant SLO.","tradeoff":"Right for a tight SLO and large traffic."},{"name":"No plan; rely on the provider","verdict":"poor","why":"Foundry has no automatic failover.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only if downtime has no business cost."}],"flips":[{"if":"The tool becomes customer-facing","then":"Re-evaluate the SLO and move to hot/warm."},{"if":"An incident exceeds 8 hours","then":"The runbook failed; invest in warm standby."}],"takeaway":"Match resilience spend to the error budget."},{"id":"w16-t11-s3","title":"A launch with five times the traffic","situation":"A launch in three weeks is expected to bring five times current traffic. Current peak is 400 RPM on PTU with Standard spillover.","constraints":["Three weeks of lead time","Spillover exists","Capacity in the region is unconfirmed"],"unknowns":["What is the measured token mix and cache rate?","How fast can quota and capacity be added?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Re-run PTU sizing at 2,000 RPM, load test, create the larger deployment early to confirm capacity, and rehearse failover","verdict":"best","why":"Capacity is not guaranteed by quota or reservation, so creating the deployment early proves it exists.","tradeoff":"Pays for capacity before launch."},{"name":"Rely on spillover for the extra load","verdict":"good","why":"No upfront cost.","tradeoff":"Standard quota may not cover 5x and latency varies."},{"name":"Buy a one-year reservation and wait","verdict":"poor","why":"Reservations do not guarantee capacity.","tradeoff":"Right after the deployment exists and utilization is known."}],"flips":[{"if":"A prefix redesign raises the cache rate","then":"Fewer PTUs are needed, so re-run the sizing."},{"if":"The launch is a one-day event","then":"Priority processing or spillover may beat a reservation."}],"takeaway":"Prove capacity before the launch, not during it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w16-t11-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Your SLO is 99.95% over a 30-day month. How many minutes of error budget do you have?","answer":21.6,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"minutes","hint":"43,200 minutes x 0.0005","explain":"(1 - 0.9995) x 43,200 = 21.6 minutes. A 40-minute outage uses 185% of that budget (derived) [[tr7]].","difficulty":2},{"id":"w16-t11-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each tier to its documented recovery time.","pairs":[{"l":"Hot/hot","r":"RTO of minutes, RPO near zero"},{"l":"Hot/warm","r":"RTO of 30 minutes to 2 hours"},{"l":"Hot/cold","r":"RTO of 2 to 8 hours"}],"explain":"These are the Foundry guidance tiers; they are approximate and you must verify them with drills [[r15]].","difficulty":1},{"id":"w16-t11-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After failover latency and cost jump. Which is the most likely cause?","options":["The secondary region starts with a cold prompt cache","Quota was reduced automatically","Retries are disabled"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prompt caches are per region, so the secondary starts cold and pays full prefill and input prices until it warms [[r25]]. Quota and retries are unrelated to the jump.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does Foundry fail over automatically?","back":"No. Build two resources in two regions with two project connections, plus Service Health alerts and availability tests."},{"front":"Error budget for 99.9% over 30 days?","back":"43.2 minutes. A 40-minute outage burns about 93% of it."},{"front":"What does failover cost that a drill may hide?","back":"A cold prompt cache, secondary quota and capacity that may be sized for tests, and state that lived in the primary region."}],"related":["w16-t05","w16-t07","w16-t10","w16-l4","w16-l5","w15-t10"],"refs":["r15","r5","r6","r2","r27","r25","tr7"]}]});
